-    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspace-settings`) manages top-level tenant policies, display metadata, session lifetimes, trash retention, export permissions, and air-gap network boundaries under strict AAL2 authentication.\n\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Light](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Workspace Settings Nested Groups](images/slice5-workspace-settings-groups-nested-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings Trash Retention](images/slice5-workspace-settings-trash-retention-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Canonical internal name of the workspace | UTF-8 String (1 to 200 characters) | Required at provisioning | Tenant root identifier in PostgreSQL RLS context |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace slug used in REST routing and browser paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 63 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable tenant boundary in database session context |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable workspace title displayed in navigation header | UTF-8 String (1 to 80 characters) | `\"Workspace\"` | Visible across topbar, breadcrumbs, and notification emails |\n| `default_project_visibility` | Baseline privacy level assigned to newly initialized projects | `\"private\"`, `\"workspace\"` | `\"private\"` | Prevents accidental cross-project disclosure in multi-team workspaces |\n| `session_idle_timeout_min` | Inactivity window before user session transitions to stale | Integer between 5 and 720 minutes | 60 | Enforces local re-authentication after inactivity period |\n| `session_absolute_timeout_min` | Maximum lifetime of an authentication session cookie | Integer between 60 and 10080 minutes | 720 (12h) | Requires full re-login upon absolute expiration |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Duration soft-deleted items remain in Trash before permanent purge | Integer between 7 and 365 days | 30 | Governs compliance data retention and recovery windows |\n| `mcp_key_max_ttl_days` | Maximum validity period permitted for minted MCP API keys | Integer between 1 and 365 days | 90 | Caps autonomous agent credential lifetimes |\n| `air_gap_mode` | Strict isolation mode blocking external cloud AI and external egress | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When active, restricts AI execution strictly to local Ollama and on-prem runners |\n| `default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers` | Agent-Safe Sequence Numbering policy toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Skips culturally and technically loaded sequence numbers (`13`, `69`, `88`, `404`, `420`, `666`, `1337`, `1488`) during issue sequence allocation |\n| `issue_export_enabled` | Allows exporting work items to JSON or CSV archives | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Controlled data exfiltration prevention |\n| `issue_export_allowed_roles` | Explicit RBAC roles permitted to export work item data | List of role strings (`\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, `\"member\"`, `\"guest\"`) | `[\"workspace_admin\", \"project_admin\", \"member\"]` | Restricts bulk export capability to authorized team members |\n| `issue_import_enabled` | Allows lossless ingestion from external trackers | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Gates administrative bulk ingestion workflows |\n| `report_export_enabled` | Allows exporting statistical flow reports and audit logs | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Governs access to compliance and telemetry exports |\n| `report_export_allowed_roles` | Roles allowed to download statistical flow telemetry | List of role strings (`\"ADMIN\"`, `\"MEMBER\"`) | Both roles | Prevents unauthorized aggregation of engineering velocity data |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workspace Settings)\n\n- **name:** Internal database name representing the workspace tenant in PostgreSQL. Required during bootstrap and immutable across standard operation, binding the tenant scope for Row-Level Security policies.\n- **slug:** URL-safe ASCII slug identifying the tenant in REST API endpoints (`/api/v1/workspaces/{slug}`) and browser URL routes (`/{slug}/projects`). Must be between 2 and 63 alphanumeric characters and hyphens.\n- **display_name:** The human-facing label rendered on topbars, page document titles, mobile shells, and notification cards. Supports UTF-8 strings up to 80 characters with full internationalization.\n- **default_project_visibility:** Sets the default privacy tier assigned when users create new projects. Options are `\"private\"` (accessible strictly to explicitly invited members and project administrators, default) or `\"workspace\"` (visible to all authenticated workspace members).\n- **session_idle_timeout_min:** Controls how long an idle user session remains active before requiring step-up authentication or PIN challenge. Configurable from 5 minutes to 720 minutes (12 hours), with a standard enterprise default of 60 minutes.\n- **session_absolute_timeout_min:** Hard maximum duration for any active session cookie regardless of user activity. Configurable between 60 minutes (1 hour) and 10080 minutes (7 days), defaulting to 720 minutes (12 hours).\n- **trash_retention_days:** Defines how many days soft-deleted work items, docs, stickies, and cycles reside in the Trash Studio (`/trash`) before being permanently purged by the background vacuum worker. Values range from 7 to 365 days (default 30 days).\n- **mcp_key_max_ttl_days:** Upper boundary enforced when administrators or developers mint Model Context Protocol (MCP) scoped tokens. The backend rejects any key creation request specifying a TTL exceeding this configured cap (1 to 365 days, default 90 days).\n- **air_gap_mode:** When set to `true`, \ud83d\udc4dkanban activates zero-external-egress enforcement. All outbound requests to cloud AI providers (OpenAI, Gemini) and remote webhooks are fail-closed blocked at the transport layer, restricting agent pipelines exclusively to LAN Ollama servers and local CLI runners.\n- **default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers:** An Agent-Safe Sequence Numbering policy flag. When enabled, the sequence allocator automatically skips culturally and technically loaded issue sequence numbers (`13`, `69`, `88`, `404`, `420`, `666`, `1337`, `1488`) during work item creation (DB/API default `false`).\n- **issue_export_enabled:** Master toggle for work item data extraction. Disabling this flag blocks `/api/v1/projects/{project}/issues/export` across both the REST API and the web interface to prevent bulk data exfiltration.\n- **issue_export_allowed_roles:** Role allowlist determining who can trigger issue exports. Configurable with combinations of `\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, `\"member\"`, and `\"guest\"`, defaulting to `[\"workspace_admin\", \"project_admin\", \"member\"]` (3 roles, excluding guest accounts).\n- **issue_import_enabled:** Administrative toggle governing data ingestion from external platforms (Plane, Jira, GitHub, Linear). Disabling this blocks `/api/v1/admin/imports` to preserve data integrity during audit periods.\n- **report_export_enabled:** Controls whether statistical flow telemetry, Monte Carlo lead-time forecasts, and velocity percentiles can be downloaded as CSV or JSON payloads.\n- **report_export_allowed_roles:** Specifies the minimum permission tier required to export engineering flow reports (options include `\"ADMIN\"` and `\"MEMBER\"`).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Configuring Workspace Policies and Trash Retention\n\n1. **Access Workspace Settings:** Click your avatar or the settings cog in the top navigation bar and select **Workspace Settings** (or navigate directly to `/workspaces/<slug>/settings`).\n2. **Review General Policies:** In the General tab, update your Display Name, verify the Workspace Slug, and select the Default Project Visibility (`Private` or `Workspace`).\n3. **Configure Session Security:** Under Session Lifetimes, configure Session Idle Timeout (e.g. 60 minutes) to force re-authentication for unattended terminals, and set Session Absolute Timeout (e.g. 720 minutes / 12 hours) to mandate periodic credential rotation.\n4. **Tune Compliance & Trash Retention:** In the Data Lifecycle & Trash card, adjust Trash Retention Days between 7 and 365 days (default 30 days). Soft-deleted work items remain recoverable in Trash for this duration before automated irreversible purging.\n5. **Manage Air-Gap and Export Restrictions:** If operating in a sovereign or disconnected environment, toggle Air-Gap Mode to ON to restrict agent and runner execution to LAN hosts. Review Allowed Export Roles to govern bulk data extraction.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **Save Settings** to persist the configuration to the database and audit ledger. Changes take effect across the workspace immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio & Personal Settings\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project workflows, custom status states, WIP limits, priority emojis, labels, and two-stage project deletion. Personal Settings (`/settings` or `/profile`) manages user profiles, canonical timezones, custom avatars, password rotation, Passkeys, TOTP 2FA, recovery codes, and external identity linking.\n\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable project display name | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in topbars and command palette |\n| `key` | Issue identifier prefix (e.g. `KAN`, `CORE`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]{1,9}$` (2 to 10 chars) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates unique issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `state_name` | Name of workflow state (e.g. `In Progress`) | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on board columns, list groupings, and peek drawers |\n| `group` | Lifecycle category mapping | `\"backlog\"`, `\"unstarted\"`, `\"started\"`, `\"completed\"`, `\"cancelled\"` | Required | Drives cycle velocity metrics, burn-downs, and SLA calculations |\n| `color_hex` | Hexadecimal color accent for state badge | 7-character hex code (`^#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$`) | `#3B82F6` | Provides distinct visual identification across board and table views |\n| `sort_order` | Execution ordering of states across the board | Integer (0 to 1000) | `0` | Controls horizontal board lane arrangement and transition flow |\n| `is_default` | Default state assigned to newly created issues | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Exactly one state per project can be designated default |\n| `wip_soft_limit` | Warning threshold for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Renders subtle amber capacity warnings when column count is exceeded |\n| `wip_hard_limit` | Strict blocking limit for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Disallows moving or creating cards into lane when limit is reached |\n| `priority_slug` | Normalized priority key (e.g. `urgent`, `high`) | Lowercase regex `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | Required | Standardized priority value used in query filters and formula AST |\n| `priority_label` | Human-readable priority label with emoji | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on card badges, priority dropdowns, and peek rails |\n| `exclusive_group` | Mutual exclusion category for label grouping | Lowercase regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | `null` (Optional) | Enforces single-selection among conflicting tags (e.g. `env:prod`) |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Project Settings)\n\n- **name:** The project name displayed across navigation breadcrumbs, issue peek drawers, and workspace summary cards. Scoped strictly to the active workspace (max 120 characters).\n- **key:** The immutable uppercase prefix key identifying work items within this project (e.g. `KAN` generates `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). Validated against `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]{1,9}$` (between 2 and 10 alphanumeric characters).\n- **state_name:** The human-facing title of a workflow state (e.g. `Backlog`, `In Progress`, `Code Review`, `Done`). Renaming a state immediately reflects across all active cards without modifying historical audit events.\n- **group:** The canonical lifecycle category to which the state belongs (`backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled`). Group mapping is fundamental: cycle burndowns, cumulative flow diagrams, and throughput percentiles aggregate work based on group identity.\n- **color_hex:** A 7-character hexadecimal color string (`#RRGGBB`) applied to column lane headers, state pills, and board drop zones to ensure high contrast and immediate readability.\n- **sort_order:** An integer specifying the left-to-right presentation order of board columns and workflow dropdowns. Lower numbers appear earlier in the delivery lifecycle.\n- **is_default:** Designates the initial workflow state automatically assigned when a contributor quick-creates an issue without specifying a state. Exactly one state per project can be default.\n- **wip_soft_limit:** An advisory Work-In-Progress limit for Kanban columns (1 to 999). When the active card count exceeds this number, the column header displays an amber count badge (`5/4 WIP`) without preventing drops.\n- **wip_hard_limit:** A strict Work-In-Progress enforcement threshold (1 to 999). When reached, further card drops or status changes targeting this column are rejected at the API boundary (`422 WIP_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`). Must be greater than or equal to `wip_soft_limit`.\n- **priority_slug:** Machine-readable identifier for priority tiers (`urgent`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `none`). Used in Safe Formula evaluations, MCP tool queries, and REST search filters.\n- **priority_label:** The user-facing priority string, typically combining an expressive semantic emoji and clear text (e.g. `\ud83d\udd34 Urgent`, `\ud83d\udfe0 High`, `\ud83d\udfe1 Medium`, `\ud83d\udd35 Low`).\n- **exclusive_group:** An optional label category constraint. When applied to tags (e.g. `tier:free`, `tier:pro`), applying one tag from the group automatically removes any previously attached tags in that same exclusive group.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Setting Project WIP Limits and Workflow States\n\n1. **Navigate to Project Settings:** Open your target project (e.g. `/projects/KAN`) and click **Settings** in the project sidebar.\n2. **Open Workflow States Studio:** Select the **States** tab to view all currently configured workflow states grouped by lifecycle phase.\n3. **Add or Edit a State:** Click **+ Add State** or click the edit icon on an existing state row. Specify the State Name (e.g. `In Review`), assign the Lifecycle Group (`started` for active work, `completed` for resolved work), choose an Accent Color, and check Make Default if newly created tickets should land in this column.\n4. **Configure WIP Limits:** Enter a Soft WIP Limit (e.g. `4`) to flag column congestion, and enter a Hard WIP Limit (e.g. `6`) if your team enforces strict pull-based flow and prohibits card overloading (`wip_soft_limit <= wip_hard_limit`).\n5. **Reorder Board Columns:** Drag states up and down using the grip handle to adjust their execution sort order from left to right.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **Save States** to persist the configuration. Board columns update immediately across all connected WebSockets.\n\n---\n\n### Personal Settings\nThe Personal Settings Studio (`/settings` or `/profile`) provides comprehensive user identity self-service, including canonical timezone localization, avatar upload with automatic sanitization and cropping, password rotation with server-admin entropy rules, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP two-factor authentication, one-time emergency recovery codes, and external OIDC/LDAP identity account linking.\n\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Light](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Personal Settings - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Personal Settings Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `display_name` | Public user profile name displayed to colleagues | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | User's full name | Sanitized against invisible Unicode characters and control codes |\n| `timezone` | Canonical IANA timezone used for date formatting | 15 canonical timezones (e.g. `America/New_York`, `UTC`) | `UTC` | Localizes ticket creation, audit timestamps, and SLA due date displays |\n| `avatar` | User profile photograph or custom avatar | PNG, JPEG, or WebP image file (<= 2 MiB) | Default letter icon | Re-encoded server-side via Pillow; SVG uploads rejected fail-closed |\n| `current_password` | Verification of existing credentials before password update | String matching current password | Required on change | Prevents unauthorized password changes on unattended sessions |\n| `new_password` | Updated local authentication password | Min 12 chars (Min 14 for Server Admins) | User-supplied | Salted and hashed via Argon2id with server-side pepper |\n| `passkeys` | FIDO2 / WebAuthn biometric security keys | Platform (Touch ID/Hello) or Cross-Platform (YubiKey) | None enrolled | Hardware-bound cryptographic public key authentication |\n| `totp_enabled` | Time-based one-time password (RFC 6238) 2FA | 6-digit verification code from authenticator app | Disabled | Requires AAL2 step-up challenge to disable once activated |\n| `recovery_codes` | Single-use emergency account recovery backup codes | 10 alphanumeric single-use codes | Generated on 2FA setup | Stored hashed in PostgreSQL; invalidated upon individual consumption |\n| `linked_identities` | External OIDC, Google, GitHub, or LDAP accounts | Verified external provider identity claims | Primary login card | Enables single-sign-on without password storage; unlinking requires AAL2 |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Personal Settings)\n\n- **display_name:** The user's public identity rendered across comments, avatar badges, activity feeds, and assignment menus. Cleaned server-side using Unicode guards to strip zero-width spaces and spoofing homoglyphs.\n- **timezone:** The user's localized timezone selection, chosen from 15 canonical IANA timezones (such as `America/New_York`, `Europe/London`, `Asia/Tokyo`, `UTC`). All timestamps remain stored canonically as UTC in PostgreSQL and are formatted to the user's local offset on the client.\n- **avatar:** Profile picture uploaded via `PUT /api/v1/auth/me/avatar`. The backend enforces a strict 2 MiB size cap and validates the raster payload via content-sniffing. Dangerous vector formats (SVG) are rejected fail-closed to eliminate XSS risks, and images are re-encoded to sanitized PNG format.\n- **current_password:** Required whenever a user modifies their local password via `POST /api/v1/auth/me/password`. Guards against session hijacking by requiring proof of current knowledge before modifying credentials.\n- **new_password:** The updated password. The platform enforces strict entropy requirements: standard users must supply at least 12 characters, while users possessing the `SERVER_ADMIN` role must supply at least 14 characters.\n- **passkeys:** WebAuthn credentials registered via `POST /api/v1/auth/webauthn/register:start` and `:finish`. Passkeys support both platform biometrics (Touch ID, Face ID, Windows Hello) and cross-platform hardware tokens (YubiKey). Each enrolled key displays nickname, enrolled date, and a non-intrusive **Test Passkey** verification button.\n- **totp_enabled:** Activates Time-Based One-Time Password verification for authenticator applications (Aegis, Google Authenticator, 1Password). Enrolled via QR code and verified with a 6-digit challenge code. Disabling TOTP requires mandatory AAL2 step-up verification.\n- **recovery_codes:** A set of 10 single-use emergency recovery codes generated when two-factor authentication is configured. Each code can be used exactly once to regain account access if hardware tokens or authenticator apps are lost.\n- **linked_identities:** Manages connected third-party enterprise identity cards (Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Okta, Active Directory LDAP). Users can link multiple external accounts to a single local identity or unlink secondary providers after completing AAL2 step-up verification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio & Git Repo Bindings\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) coordinates lifecycle rules, pre-transition validators, field mutators, asynchronous AI agent gates, and read-only bare Git repository mirror bindings.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workflow-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive label for transition helper hook | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | Required | Displayed in pipeline lists, dry-run logs, and error explanations |\n| `from_state` | Origin workflow state of the transition rule | Valid state name or `null` (Any state) | `null` (Wildcard) | Restricts hook evaluation to specific card movements |\n| `to_state` | Destination workflow state of the transition | Valid project state name | Required | The target column being protected by the transition helper |\n| `hook_kind` | Execution engine and evaluation timing | `\"sync_validator\"`, `\"sync_mutator\"`, `\"agent_interceptor\"` | `\"sync_validator\"` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if helper times out or rejects | `\"fail_closed\"`, `\"fail_open_warn\"` | `\"fail_closed\"` | Fail-closed guarantees invalid transitions never commit |\n| `wasm_module_sha256` | SHA-256 digest of sandboxed Wasm module | 64-character lowercase hex string | Required for Wasm | Verifies bytecode authenticity before in-process execution |\n| `agent` | Identifier of registered autonomous agent | Valid agent slug (e.g. `reality-checker`) | Required for Agent | Binds asynchronous gate to configured LLM or CLI runner harness |\n| `timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed execution time in milliseconds | Integer between 1 and 300000 ms | 60000 for Agent | Protects transition queues from hanging subprocesses |\n| `allowed_mutable_fields` | Explicit field allowlist for mutator hooks | List of field names (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, `custom_fields.<slug>`) | `[]` (Empty) | Enforces least-privilege mutation; `state` and `description` are forbidden |\n| `repo_slug` | Identifier for project bare Git mirror | Regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$` | Required | Names local bare mirror directory under `/var/lib/kanban/repos/` |\n| `remote_url` | Upstream Git repository clone URL | HTTPS or SSH Git URL | Required | Validated against SSRF policy before initiating background fetch |\n| `default_ref` | Primary branch or ref used for agent diff inspections | Valid Git ref name (e.g. `main`, `master`) | `\"main\"` | Baseline target branch used by reality-checker audits |\n| `access_mode` | Storage access mode for repository mirror | `\"ro_vfs_mount\"`, `\"bare_git_tools\"` | `\"ro_vfs_mount\"` | Enforces read-only filesystem or git tool execution |\n| `credential` | Optional credential reference for private repository authentication | `null`, `\"workspace-pat\"`, `\"ssh-key\"` | `null` | Secure token reference stored in credentials vault |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workflow Transition Studio & Repo Bindings)\n\n- **name:** The user-defined identifier assigned to the workflow hook (such as `qa-done-gate` or `enforce-pr-link`, 1 to 120 characters). Appears on pipeline transition cards, dry-run evaluation outputs, and timeline rejection entries.\n- **from_state:** The initial state of the card before transition (e.g. `In Progress`). Selecting `null` creates a wildcard rule matching transitions originating from any state into `to_state`.\n- **to_state:** The protected destination state (e.g. `Done`). The card cannot land in this state until all attached synchronous validators pass and all asynchronous agent gates approve.\n- **hook_kind:** Defines the execution engine: `\"sync_validator\"` runs fast in-process WebAssembly checks (<25ms); `\"sync_mutator\"` applies deterministic property updates; `\"agent_interceptor\"` dispatches an asynchronous background gate to an AI model or test runner.\n- **failure_policy:** The error handling invariant. `\"fail_closed\"` (default) blocks the transition immediately if the helper fails, times out, or errors. `\"fail_open_warn\"` logs an administrative warning to the activity ledger and allows the card transition to complete.\n- **wasm_module_sha256:** The cryptographic hash of the compiled WebAssembly bytecode module executed by the `wasmtime` engine. Prevents execution of unverified or tampered binaries.\n- **agent:** The slug of the registered autonomous agent (`@reality-checker`, `@security-auditor`) assigned to evaluate asynchronous gate transitions. The attach dialog groups available agents by execution harness (`cli_pipe`, `in_app_sdk`, `remote_runner`).\n- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock execution deadline allocated to the helper (validating between 1 and 300,000 ms, defaulting to 60,000 ms for agent interceptors).\n- **allowed_mutable_fields:** A strict security allowlist restricting which issue properties a `sync_mutator` hook can modify (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, and `custom_fields.<slug>`). Project invariants strictly forbid modifying `state` or `description_markdown` to prevent recursive transition loops and content tampering.\n- **repo_slug:** The local filesystem folder name allocated to the bare Git mirror. Must be a clean lowercase alphanumeric string with hyphens.\n- **remote_url:** The external Git repository URL (e.g. `https://github.com/org/repo.git`). The server validates the URL against an anti-SSRF policy (blocking RFC 1918 private IP addresses unless explicitly allowlisted) before establishing outbound connections.\n- **default_ref:** The primary branch or ref name against which code inspection scripts, PR validation checks, and diff analyses are evaluated (default `\"main\"`).\n- **access_mode:** Defines how agents access repository contents: `\"ro_vfs_mount\"` mounts a read-only filesystem tree, while `\"bare_git_tools\"` allows executing read-only Git query commands.\n- **credential:** Optional secret identifier (`\"workspace-pat\"` or `\"ssh-key\"`) used to authenticate against private upstream repositories without exposing cleartext credentials to agents.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Attaching Workflow Transition Hooks with Dry-Run\n\n1. **Navigate to Workflow Studio:** Open `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows`.\n2. **Select Transition Rule:** Locate the transition rule card for your target state movement (e.g. `In Progress -> Done`).\n3. **Click Attach Hook:** Click the **+ Attach Hook** button to open the Attach Workflow Hook Dialog.\n4. **Define Hook Parameters:** Specify a unique Hook Name (e.g. `reality-checker-done-gate`), select the Hook Kind (`sync_validator`, `sync_mutator`, or `agent_interceptor`), and choose the Failure Policy (`fail_closed` to block unverified tickets, or `fail_open_warn` to log warnings while allowing progress).\n5. **Select Engine or Agent:** For Wasm, choose an approved module (e.g. `check-blockers-resolved`). For Agent Interceptors, select the target Agent Definition from the dropdown; the system automatically populates the agent's real timeout (between 1 and 300,000 ms, e.g. `60000ms`). For mutators, select permitted mutable fields (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, or `custom_fields.<slug>`).\n6. **Save Hook:** Click **Attach Hook** to commit the hook to the project's transition pipeline.\n7. **Simulate in Dry-Run Drawer:** Click the **Dry-Run** button on the rule card, choose a sample ticket fixture or enter a live issue key, click **Run Dry-Run** to execute the pipeline in a simulated, non-mutating sandbox, and inspect the execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms`, `3.4ms`), returned logs, and allowed/vetoed verdicts before deploying to production.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Binding Git Repositories\n\n1. **Access Repository Bindings:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows` and scroll down to the Git Repository Bindings section.\n2. **Click Bind Repository:** Click **+ Bind Git Repository** to open the repository configuration drawer.\n3. **Configure Repository Details:** Enter the Repository Slug (e.g. `kanban-core`), remote repository URL (`remote_url`), default tracking branch or ref (`default_ref`, e.g. `main`), access mode (`ro_vfs_mount` or `bare_git_tools`), and optional credential binding (`workspace-pat` or `ssh-key`).\n4. **Enforce Read-Only Mirror Invariant:** The system creates a local bare Git mirror with strict read-only permissions (`:ro`). Autonomous agents can query commit trees and run `git diff` during gate reviews, but write access is impossible.\n5. **Trigger Immediate Mirror Refresh:** Click **Refresh Mirror** to perform an initial fetch and verify that commit SHAs and branch references synchronize successfully.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) manages dynamic attributes, safe mathematical formulas, WebAssembly modules, and dual-control tenant scope escalations under AAL2 cryptographic verification.\n\n![Custom Fields Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Custom Fields Editor Tier A](images/slice5-custom-fields-editor-tier-a-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Structured Format](images/slice5-custom-fields-structured-format-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Dual Control Escalation](images/slice5-custom-fields-escalation-dual-control-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable custom field title | String (1 to 80 characters) | Required | Displayed on ticket rails, table columns, and board badges |\n| `slug` | Unique identifier used in formulas and API queries | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and underlying calculation engine | `\"scalar\"`, `\"formula\"`, `\"wasm\"`, `\"nsjail\"` | `\"scalar\"` | Determines execution latency and Standalone mode compatibility |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `\"scalar\"`, `\"wasm\"` (Tier A), `\"nsjail\"` (Tier B) | `\"wasm\"` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Identity token provided during script execution | `\"INVOKING_USER\"`, `\"PROJECT_ADMIN_SP\"`, `\"SERVER_ADMIN_SP\"` | `\"INVOKING_USER\"` | Elevated service principal modes require dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `\"PROJECT\"`, `\"WORKSPACE\"`, `\"SERVER\"` | `\"PROJECT\"` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `\"text\"`, `\"number\"`, `\"badge\"`, `\"severity_pill\"`, `\"url\"`, `\"markdown_snippet\"`, `\"json_table\"` | `\"text\"` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen format | JSON object matching format schema | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Line-delimited (`\\n`) domain list | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n| `approval_status` | Current dual-control escalation state | `\"PENDING\"`, `\"PENDING_SECOND_APPROVER\"`, `\"APPROVED\"`, `\"REVOKED\"` | `\"APPROVED\"` (Project) | Gated at `PENDING` when requesting Workspace or Server scope |\n| `aal2_freshness` | Maximum age of AAL2 verification for approval | Integer (Max 900 seconds / 15 minutes) | 900s | Enforces fresh multi-factor authentication on administrative step-up |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation)\n\n- **name:** The human-facing label displayed on ticket property rails, table view columns, and board card badges (1 to 80 characters). Rendered alongside a subtle test tube icon (`\ud83e\uddea`) for computed fields.\n- **slug:** The immutable ASCII identifier representing the custom field in JSON payloads, query filters, and Safe Formula references. Derived automatically from the field name upon creation.\n- **flavor:** The computation tier. UI defaults to `\"scalar\"` (`CustomFieldDefinitionEditor.tsx` line 245; stores static values); `\"formula\"` executes deterministic Python AST math in-process (<5ms); `\"wasm\"` runs compiled WebAssembly modules via `wasmtime` (<25ms; API tier default); `\"nsjail\"` executes Linux container scripts (<250ms).\n- **sandbox_tier:** Selects between in-process WebAssembly (`wasm` Tier A) and containerized namespace isolation (`nsjail` Tier B). In Standalone mode, `nsjail` is fail-closed disabled with `SANDBOX_UNAVAILABLE_IN_STANDALONE`.\n- **credential_mode:** Determines which identity credentials are provided to the script during execution. `\"INVOKING_USER\"` executes strictly within the permissions of the calling user. `\"PROJECT_ADMIN_SP\"` executes with Project Admin service principal authority. `\"SERVER_ADMIN_SP\"` executes with Server Admin service principal authority and mandates dual-control approval.\n- **target_scope:** Controls the data visibility boundary of the field. `\"PROJECT\"` scope can only read issues within the active project. Escalating to `\"WORKSPACE\"` (reading across all workspace projects) or `\"SERVER\"` (global) requires dual-control cryptographic sign-off.\n- **output_format:** The strict Pydantic schema used to validate and render the field's computed output. Supports 7 canonical formats: `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, and `json_table`.\n- **format_metadata:** A JSON configuration dictionary customizing the chosen output format (such as specifying `unit_suffix: \"%\"`, `maximum_fraction_digits: 1`, or table column definitions).\n- **script_source:** The executable source code (Python formula expression, Wasm bytecode, or shell script). Hashed with SHA-256 upon save; if the hash in PostgreSQL fails verification at runtime, execution is halted immediately.\n- **egress_allowlist:** A line-delimited (`\\n`) list of permitted network hosts accessible to `nsjail` scripts. By default, network egress is completely disabled via kernel `CLONE_NEWNET`.\n- **approval_status:** Tracks the dual-control lifecycle (`PENDING`, `PENDING_SECOND_APPROVER`, `APPROVED`, `REVOKED`). Fields requesting `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER` scope enter `PENDING` status and cannot execute until approved by an administrator holding target tier authority.\n- **aal2_freshness:** The security policy requiring that the approving administrator must have performed multi-factor authentication (biometric Passkey, hardware key, or TOTP) within the preceding 900 seconds (15 minutes).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Authoring and Requesting Dual-Control Custom Scripted Fields\n\n1. **Open Custom Fields Studio:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/custom-fields` and click **+ New Custom Field**.\n2. **Define Field Metadata:** Enter a Name (e.g. `Cross-Project Security Compliance Score`), confirm the automatically generated Slug (`cross-proj-sec-score`), select the Flavor (`formula` or `wasm`), and choose the Output Format (`severity_pill` or `number`).\n3. **Request Elevated Scope:** Set Target Scope to `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER`. Optionally select Credential Mode as `PROJECT_ADMIN_SP` or `SERVER_ADMIN_SP` if elevated service authority is required. The UI immediately surfaces an amber Dual-Control Escalation Banner warning of the expanded data access boundary.\n4. **Draft and Test Execute:** Author your formula expression or script logic, then click **Test Execute** to simulate evaluation in memory against sample payloads.\n5. **Submit for Approval:** Click **Save & Request Escalation**. The field enters `pending` approval status.\n6. **Independent AAL2 Review & Approval:** A distinct Workspace or Server Administrator opens the Custom Fields Studio, inspects the script source, requested scope, and blast radius, clicks **Approve Escalation**, and provides fresh Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials (Passkey, security key, or TOTP). Once verified, the field is activated and logged in the immutable audit ledger.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) and Granular RBAC Policy Builder configure enterprise identity providers, Active Directory / LDAPS trees, OpenID Connect SSO, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP multi-factor authentication, break-glass lockout prevention, and 30-permission custom roles.\n\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice1-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_kind` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake, secret custody, and token exchange |\n| `slug` | URL-safe card identifier used in auth routes | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` (3 to 48 chars) | Required | Formats callback and login endpoints (e.g. `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`) |\n| `display_name` | User-facing card title rendered on `/login` screen | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed directly on login buttons and cards |\n| `button_subtext` | Optional explanatory subtext beneath button | UTF-8 String (up to 200 characters) or null | None | Guides users on credential format or organizational tenant |\n| `icon_preset` | Visual icon rendered inside login card button | `key`, `shield`, `google`, `github`, `microsoft`, `ldap`, `terminal` | `key` | Provides immediate visual recognition on `/login` container |\n| `is_enabled` | Master active state toggle for the card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Disabled cards reject login attempts immediately with 403 FORBIDDEN |\n| `sort_rank` | Fractional rank ordering on `/login` screen | Fractional-index midpoint string (e.g. `\"V00V\"`, `\"kV0FV\"`) computed via `midpoint_rank()` | Derived | Lower ranks appear first; allows prioritizing corporate SSO over fallback admin |\n| `auto_provision_users` | Automatic local user creation on first SSO login | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When true, new users are provisioned in workspace with default role |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Domain allowlist for user registration and SSO | Array of domain strings (e.g. `[\"corp.example.com\"]`) | Empty list (Allow all) | Rejects unmatched email claims during OIDC and LDAP authentication |\n| `public_config` | Non-sensitive provider configuration dictionary | JSON object | `{}` | Exposes discovery URLs and client IDs without leaking secrets |\n| `secret` | Sensitive client secret or bind password | Write-only string | None | Encrypted at rest via AES-256-GCM envelope encryption (`MASTER_KEK`) |\n| `google_oauth_json` | Google Cloud Console client secret JSON | Valid JSON string (up to 32,768 characters) | None | Quick-import payload automatically parsed and verified by backend |\n\n\n### Active Directory and LDAPS Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `url` | Directory connection URL with scheme and host | `^(ldaps\\|ldap)://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]{1,5})?$` | Required | Plaintext unencrypted LDAP rejected; requires `ldaps://` or `ldap://` with StartTLS |\n| `transport_mode` | Cryptographic transport security protocol | `ldaps` (implicit TLS on port 636), `starttls` (explicit StartTLS on port 389) | `ldaps` | Enforces TLS handshake before transmitting bind credentials |\n| `ca_cert_pem` | Custom enterprise Certificate Authority bundle | PEM-formatted X.509 certificate string (max 64 KiB) | None | Pins enterprise root CA; rejects untrusted self-signed certificates |\n| `spki_sha256_pins` | Subject Public Key Info SHA-256 pin hashes | Array of 64-hex SHA-256 strings (up to 4 pins) | `[]` | Cryptographically pins public key identity; prevents rogue CA issuance |\n| `service_bind_dn` | Service account Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (up to 512 chars) or null | None | Read-only service principal used for user lookup and group searches |\n| `base_dn` | Directory search subtree root Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (1 to 512 chars) | Required | Scopes user search to authorized organizational container (e.g. `OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=com`) |\n| `user_search_filter` | LDAP query filter for user account resolution | LDAP filter expression substituting `{username}` | `(&(objectClass=person)(uid={username}))` | Resolves login username to directory user; AD preset uses `sAMAccountName` |\n| `subject_attribute` | Attribute providing immutable user identity | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `entryUUID`, `objectGUID`) | `entryUUID` | Binds external user identity immutably across username renames |\n| `email_attribute` | Attribute containing user email address | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `mail`) | `mail` | Source of verified user email address for workspace mapping |\n| `display_name_attribute` | Attribute containing user full name | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `displayName`, `cn`) | `cn` | Displayed on user profile and activity ledger |\n| `member_of_attribute` | Attribute containing direct group memberships | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `memberOf`) | `memberOf` | Evaluated during group membership extraction and role mapping |\n| `group_dn_map` | Dictionary mapping directory group DNs to roles | JSON dictionary of `{dn: role_slug}` (up to 200 mappings) | `{}` | Automatically provisions mapped workspace and project roles upon login |\n| `access_mode` | Access restriction policy | `any_user` (all valid users), `group_restricted` (member of allowed group) | `any_user` | When restricted, users not in `user_groups` or `admin_groups` are denied entry |\n| `admin_groups` | Directory group names granting admin authority | Array of group names (up to 200 chars each) | `[]` | Grants `SERVER_ADMIN` or `WORKSPACE_ADMIN` to authenticated group members |\n| `user_groups` | Directory group names granting member authority | Array of group names (up to 200 chars each) | `[]` | Grants baseline member access when `access_mode` is `group_restricted` |\n\n\n### OpenID Connect (OIDC) and Google OAuth Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint base URL | Valid HTTPS URL | Required | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic JWKS keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth application client identifier | ASCII string | Required | Identifies application to identity provider authorization server |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth application client secret | Write-only secret string | Required | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never returned in REST responses |\n| `scopes` | OIDC OAuth scope parameters | Array of scope strings in API; comma-separated in drawer | `[\"openid\", \"email\", \"profile\"]` | Must include `openid` and `email` for verified claim extraction |\n| `redirect_uri` | Callback endpoint registered with IdP | Fixed format `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback` | Derived | Enforces strict origin matching against current deployment URL |\n| `require_email_verified` | Rejects unverified IdP email addresses | Boolean (`true`) | `true` | If `email_verified: false`, rejects authentication with 403 EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED |\n\n\n### Personal Security and Multi-Factor Authentication Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | Canonical string `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or relay attacks |\n| `totp_secret` | High-entropy base32 secret for TOTP | Base32 string (RFC 6238) | Generated on enroll | Validates 6-digit one-time codes with 30s step interval and +/- 1 drift |\n| `recovery_codes` | Backup emergency recovery codes | Array of 10 single-use high-entropy recovery codes | 10 codes | Single-use emergency codes stored as salted hashes for break-glass recovery |\n| `websudo_duration_seconds` | Duration of elevated AAL2 administrator session | Integer (seconds) | `900` (15 minutes) | Sliding window for sensitive mutations; explicit drop via `/aal2/drop` |\n| `lockout_prevention_layer` | Anti-lockout verification enforcement | `db_trigger`, `handshake_probe_15m` | Both active | Blocks disabling LOCAL_ADMIN without verified interactive admin login within 15m |\n\n\n### Granular RBAC Policy Builder Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `rbac_scope_tier` | Administrative scope tier of the role | `system admin`, `workspace admin`, `project admin` | `workspace admin` | Governs role hierarchy and locks lower-tier admins from editing parent roles |\n| `role_slug` | Unique lowercase identifier for custom role | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` | Required | Identifies role in database bindings and audit ledger records |\n| `role_name` | Human-readable role title | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed on user management surfaces, member cards, and badges |\n| `color_hex` | UI badge accent color | 7-character hex code (e.g. `#4f46e5`) | `#6b7280` | Distinguishes role visually across member tables and peek drawers |\n| `rbac_permissions` | List of granted granular permissions | Array of permission strings from 30 canonical keys | `[]` | Evaluated with explicit DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`) |\n\n![Custom RBAC Policy Builder - Desktop](images/slice1-custom-rbac-policy-builder.png)\n\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **provider_kind:** Specifies the authentication protocol implemented by the card. Permitted values are `LOCAL_ADMIN` (argon2id hashed platform administrative credentials), `OIDC` (OpenID Connect discovery and authorization code flow), `OAUTH2` (generic OAuth 2.0 provider with userinfo endpoint), and `LDAP` (Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration). The provider kind determines schema validation rules, secret custody requirements, and token minting behavior.\n- **slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the authentication card. Must conform to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` between 3 and 48 characters. The slug forms the path parameter in public login and callback routes, including `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`, `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback`, and `/api/v1/auth/ldap/{slug}/login`. Immutable after creation to maintain bookmark and redirect stability.\n- **display_name:** The user-facing label rendered on the login card button on `/login` (1 to 100 characters). For example, `\"Corporate Active Directory\"` or `\"Google Workspace SSO\"`. Provides clear identification of the identity source for end users.\n- **button_subtext:** Optional secondary guidance rendered in smaller typography directly below the card title (up to 200 characters). Commonly used to specify login formats, such as `\"Use your firstname.lastname@example.com account\"` or `\"Hardware security key required\"`.\n- **icon_preset:** Visual icon token rendered on the authentication card button. Supported values include `key` (generic credential), `shield` (security key / passkey), `google` (Google colored G), `github` (GitHub octocat), `microsoft` (Windows logo), `ldap` (directory tree), and `terminal` (administrative root). Enhances visual scannability on multi-card login surfaces.\n- **is_enabled:** Master toggle controlling whether the authentication card is active on the `/login` screen. When set to `false`, the card is hidden from the login container, and any authentication attempts targeting the card's routes are immediately rejected with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN`. Mutations toggling `is_enabled` enforce anti-lockout validation.\n- **sort_rank:** Fractional-index midpoint string computed via `midpoint_rank()` (e.g. `\"V00V\"`, `\"kV0FV\"`) governing the visual sequence of cards on the `/login` container. Cards with lower lexicographical ranks appear first. Administrators use this setting to position primary corporate SSO providers at the top while keeping break-glass local admin cards accessible at the bottom without needing to renumber surrounding cards.\n- **auto_provision_users:** When enabled (`true`), an authenticated user whose external identity claim is verified but does not yet have an account in \ud83d\udc4dkanban is automatically provisioned with a new local user record and assigned the workspace default role. When disabled (`false`), external users must be explicitly invited before logging in.\n- **allowed_email_domains:** A security restriction array of permitted corporate email domains (e.g., `[\"corp.example.com\", \"subsidiary.com\"]`). During OIDC token exchange or LDAP authentication, if the user's verified email address domain does not match any entry in this list, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `DOMAIN_NOT_ALLOWED`.\n- **public_config:** A JSON dictionary containing non-sensitive provider configuration attributes. Stored in PostgreSQL `jsonb` columns and safe to expose to administrative API callers. Includes discovery URLs, client IDs, LDAP hosts, user search filters, and group mapping definitions.\n- **secret:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private key strings. Inbound payloads accept this write-only parameter, which is immediately encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM envelope encryption with the platform `MASTER_KEK`. The secret is never returned in any REST API response or serialization model.\n- **google_oauth_json:** A convenience payload accepting the raw client secret JSON file downloaded directly from the Google Cloud Console (up to 32,768 characters). The backend parses the JSON, validates client ID, client secret, redirect URI alignment, and populates `public_config` without manual copy-pasting.\n- **url:** The directory connection URL with protocol scheme and hostname matching `^(ldaps|ldap)://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]{1,5})?$`. Unencrypted plaintext LDAP is strictly rejected; connections must negotiate TLS via `ldaps://` or `ldap://` with StartTLS.\n- **transport_mode:** Transport encryption protocol for directory traffic. Permitted values are `ldaps` (direct TLS handshake upon TCP socket connection on port 636) and `starttls` (explicit StartTLS extended operation negotiation on port 389 prior to issuing bind requests).\n- **ca_cert_pem:** Custom PEM-formatted X.509 certificate authority bundle (up to 64 KiB). When connecting to enterprise directory controllers utilizing private internal PKI, administrators paste the issuing root and intermediate CA certificates here. The LDAP client validates the server certificate strictly against this bundle, rejecting untrusted certificates.\n- **spki_sha256_pins:** Array of Subject Public Key Info (SPKI) SHA-256 hashes (each exactly 64 hexadecimal characters, up to 4 pins). Configured via backend API to cryptographically pin the server certificate public key and block rogue CA or proxy interception.\n- **service_bind_dn:** The Distinguished Name of the service account used by \ud83d\udc4dkanban to execute user lookup and group resolution queries (e.g., `CN=KanbanService,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=com`, up to 512 characters). Must possess read-only search permissions over the target user and group subtrees.\n- **base_dn:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree searched for user accounts (e.g., `OU=Employees,DC=corp,DC=com`, up to 512 characters). Scopes directory queries to prevent scanning unrelated machine or organizational containers.\n- **user_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to resolve the input login username to a specific Active Directory user object. The token `{username}` is safely escaped and substituted into the filter (default `(&(objectClass=person)(uid={username}))`, Active Directory preset `(&(objectCategory=person)(objectClass=user)(sAMAccountName={username}))`).\n- **subject_attribute:** The directory attribute providing an immutable, permanent identifier for the authenticated user (default `entryUUID`, Active Directory uses `objectGUID`). Ensures account continuity even if usernames or email addresses change.\n- **email_attribute:** The directory attribute containing the user's primary email address (default `mail`). Populates the verified user email address upon authentication.\n- **display_name_attribute:** The directory attribute containing the user's human-readable name (default `cn`, Active Directory uses `displayName`). Populates the user's profile display name.\n- **member_of_attribute:** The directory attribute containing direct group memberships on user objects (default `memberOf`). Used during group synchronization to locate groups mapped to \ud83d\udc4dkanban roles.\n- **group_dn_map:** JSON dictionary mapping directory group Distinguished Names to \ud83d\udc4dkanban role slugs (up to 200 mappings). During login, the backend evaluates user group memberships and assigns mapped roles automatically.\n- **access_mode:** Access restriction policy governing directory logins. Permitted values are `any_user` (permits all successfully bound directory users) and `group_restricted` (restricts login to users belonging to specified `user_groups` or `admin_groups`).\n- **admin_groups:** Array of directory group names granting administrative privileges (`SERVER_ADMIN` or `WORKSPACE_ADMIN`). Users belonging to these groups receive elevated administrative access upon login.\n- **user_groups:** Array of directory group names granting member privileges. When `access_mode` is set to `group_restricted`, users must belong to at least one group listed in `user_groups` or `admin_groups` to log in.\n- **issuer_url:** The standard OpenID Connect discovery base URL (e.g., `https://accounts.google.com` or `https://keycloak.corp.example.com/realms/enterprise`). The backend appends `/.well-known/openid-configuration` to retrieve endpoint URLs, signing algorithms, and JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS).\n- **client_id:** The public OAuth client identifier issued by the identity provider during application registration.\n- **client_secret:** The confidential OAuth client secret credential issued by the identity provider. Held strictly in write-only custody and encrypted via AES-256-GCM.\n- **scopes:** Array of requested OAuth scopes in API payloads (`[\"openid\", \"email\", \"profile\"]`), entered as a comma-separated string in the editor drawer. Scopes must guarantee delivery of the `sub` and `email` claims.\n- **redirect_uri:** The immutable callback URI derived from the deployment hostname and card slug: `https://<domain>/api/v1/auth/oidc/<slug>/callback`. Must be registered verbatim in the identity provider console.\n- **require_email_verified:** Mandatory boolean safety check. When true, the ID token claim `email_verified` must be strictly `true`. If the claim is `false` or missing, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED` to prevent email spoofing attacks.\n- **rp_name:** WebAuthn Relying Party display name. Hardcoded to the canonical platform brand `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` across all WebAuthn registration and authentication credential requests. Displayed by operating system biometric dialogs and hardware tokens.\n- **rp_id:** WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier. Formatted as a strict ASCII Fully Qualified Domain Name matching the browser window origin without protocol, port, or trailing slashes (e.g., `kanban.example.com`). Prevents cross-domain credential phishing.\n- **totp_secret:** High-entropy base32 encoded secret key generated during TOTP authenticator enrollment (`POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/enroll`). Encrypted at rest and used to validate 6-digit RFC 6238 time-step codes with a 30-second interval and +/- 1 step drift tolerance.\n- **recovery_codes:** Array of 10 single-use backup recovery codes minted during MFA enrollment (`RECOVERY_CODE_COUNT = 10`). Stored in PostgreSQL exclusively as cryptographic salted hashes. Used for break-glass account recovery when hardware tokens or authenticator apps are unavailable.\n- **websudo_duration_seconds:** The lifetime of an elevated Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) session window (default 900 seconds / 15 minutes). All administrative endpoints (`/api/v1/admin/*`) require active AAL2 verification. When expired, the frontend prompts for step-up elevation via WebSudo without discarding dirty form state.\n- **lockout_prevention_layer:** Multi-layered defense preventing administrative lockouts. Evaluated at both the PostgreSQL trigger layer (`db_trigger`) and application service layer (`handshake_probe_15m`). While test handshakes (`POST /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/test-handshake`) verify endpoint health status, disabling or deleting `LOCAL_ADMIN` strictly requires that an external provider card has an active administrator account mapping AND has recorded a verified interactive `SERVER_ADMIN` login within the last 15 minutes (`last_admin_handshake_at >= clock_timestamp() - interval '15 minutes'`).\n- **rbac_scope_tier:** The administrative boundary defining where a custom RBAC role applies. Allowed values are `system admin` (global platform governance), `workspace admin` (workspace-wide settings, members, and custom fields), and `project admin` (project-specific workflows, WIP limits, and issue operations). Roles in higher tiers cannot be edited by administrators in lower tiers.\n- **role_slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the custom role, conforming to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$`. Identifies the role across role bindings and audit records.\n- **role_name:** The human-readable name of the custom role (1 to 100 characters), rendered in member lists, access matrices, and role assignment pills.\n- **color_hex:** Hexadecimal color string (e.g., `#4f46e5`) utilized for UI badges and role chips across user management and permission tables.\n- **rbac_permissions:** The set of granular permissions assigned to a custom RBAC role, selected from the 30 canonical keys across Workspace (8), Issue (10), and Field (12) tiers. Evaluated alongside the 8-bit capability bitmask with absolute DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`).\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Minting Modal](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `label` | Human-readable label identifying the key or client tool | String (1 to 120 characters) | None (Optional) | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `owner_kind` | Identity persona bound to minted key | `personal_user`, `service_bot` | `personal_user` | Service bot keys enforce strict service account RLS boundaries |\n| `scope_tier` | Tenancy authorization scope boundary | `personal`, `project`, `workspace`, `global` | `workspace` | Restricts object graph access to specified tenant boundary |\n| `expires_in_days` | Key validity lifespan in days | `30`, `60`, `90`, `365`, `0` (Unlimited) | `60` (Studio mint) / `30` (Personal OAuth) | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED`; Unlimited requires explicit risk confirmation |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (0 to 255) | `31` (UI default) / `7` (API default) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 0 rejects all tools with `INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY` |\n| `allowed_tools` | Explicit whitelist of accessible tools | List of tool name strings | None (All tools in surface) | Restricts tool visibility and invocation to specified set |\n| `disabled_tools` | Explicit blacklist of prohibited tools | List of tool name strings | `[]` (Empty) | Blocks invocation of specified tools even if permitted by surface |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n![Connect OAuth App Modal](images/slice3-connect-oauth-modal.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`surface_type`:** Selects the target streamable MCP endpoint. `/mcp/agent` exposes 10 execution and QA tools with a strict token ceiling of <= 1,850 tokens. `/mcp/docs` exposes 3 knowledge and documentation tools capped at <= 1,350 tokens. `/mcp/admin` exposes 12 governance and lifecycle tools capped at <= 2,800 tokens, requiring AAL2 administrative credentials.\n- **`label`:** Human-readable label assigned to the key during creation (1 to 120 characters). Surfaced in audit log feeds, active session inspectors, and the visual key management table to identify the calling entity or developer machine.\n- **`owner_kind`:** Selects between `personal_user` (executing on behalf of an authenticated human user) and `service_bot` (executing as an autonomous background bot). Service bot keys execute under dedicated service accounts with explicit RLS principal contexts.\n- **`scope_tier`:** Binds the minted token to a specific tenant boundary: `personal` (bound to current user), `project` (scoped to a single project key like `KAN`), `workspace` (scoped to all projects in workspace), or `global` (cross-workspace server scope). Project-scoped keys cannot query or mutate tickets in adjacent projects.\n- **`expires_in_days`:** Key expiration duration in days. The UI offers presets for 30 days, 60 days (default in Studio mint modal), 90 days, 365 days (1 year), and `0` (Unlimited). Selecting `Unlimited` triggers an explicit security risk warning checkbox (`confirm_unlimited`) that must be acknowledged before the key can be minted. Expired keys are rejected with `401 UNAUTHORIZED`.\n- **`capability_mask`:** Monotonic 8-bit integer capability bitmask ceiling (0 to 255). A value of `0` (`NONE`) creates a deny-all key where all tool calls are rejected with `isError: true` (`INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY`). A value of `1` (`VIEW`) restricts operations to read-only, `31` (`VIEW | COMMENT | EDIT | TRANSITION | LINK`) grants standard member permissions (UI default), and `255` (`ALL`) enables complete operational privileges. The backend API default is `7` (`VIEW | COMMENT | EDIT`). Enforced via PostgreSQL `SET LOCAL app.credential_boundary`.\n- **`allowed_tools`:** Optional explicit tool allowlist. When specified, only tools in this list may be listed or invoked by the client. Any invocation of unlisted tools is rejected with `TOOL_NOT_PERMITTED`.\n- **`disabled_tools`:** Explicit tool denylist (default empty). Specific tools can be disabled on a per-key basis (e.g. disabling `transition_ticket` for read/comment bots) even if permitted by surface and capability mask.\n- **`allowed_cidrs`:** Optional network boundary allowlist containing comma- or whitespace-separated IPv4 or IPv6 subnets (e.g. `127.0.0.1/32`, `192.168.1.0/24`). Requests originating outside allowed CIDR blocks are rejected immediately with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN_CIDR`.\n\n![MCP Config Snippet and Client Onboarding Modal](images/slice3-mcp-config-snippet-modal.png)\n\n### Connect OAuth App and Client Snippets Modals\n\n- **Connect OAuth App Modal:** Provides one-click visual integration guides and connection snippets for external AI tools:\n  - **Gemini App:** Generates direct OAuth Resource URLs for Google Gemini Connected Apps (`gemini.google.com -> Settings -> Connected Apps -> Add Custom MCP Server`).\n  - **Claude Code & Desktop:** Emits standard `claude_desktop_config.json` stanzas and `claude mcp add` CLI commands.\n  - **VS Code & Cursor:** Emits `mcpServers` configuration blocks with automatic SSE endpoint binding.\n  - **Antigravity CLI:** Emits `agy mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n  - **OpenAI Codex:** Emits `codex mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n- **MCP Config Snippet Modal:** Generates copy-paste configuration snippets for newly minted static API keys across Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, and OpenAI Codex. Includes a write-only custody warning reminding operators that secrets will never be displayed again.\n\n![Workload OIDC Federation Studio](images/slice3-workload-oidc-studio.png)\n\n### Workload Identity Federation Studio (`/settings/mcp` and `/admin/mcp`)\n\nThe Workload Identity Federation Studio allows administrators to author and manage RFC 8693 token exchange policies for headless CI/CD runners:\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive policy identifier | String (1 to 120 characters) | Required | Displayed in workload policy table and audit ledger |\n| `description` | Human-readable explanation of policy purpose | String (up to 500 characters) or None | None | Documents workload integration context and team ownership |\n| `issuer` | OpenID Connect token issuer URL | Strict HTTPS URL (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Validates incoming JWT `iss` claim; plaintext HTTP rejected |\n| `client_id` | Expected audience claim | String (1 to 255 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `aud` claim |\n| `subject_pattern` | Subject matching pattern | Glob or Regex (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `sub` claim (e.g. `repo:org/repo:*`) |\n| `required_claims_json` | Exact claim match constraints | JSON Object | `{}` | Enforces additional claims (e.g. `{\"environment\": \"production\"}`) |\n| `surface` | Target MCP surface | `agent` or `docs` | `agent` | Determines which MCP endpoint the federated token can invoke |\n| `scopes` | Allowed OAuth privilege scopes | List of `SURFACE_SCOPES` strings | `[]` (Empty) | Bounds the granted scopes (e.g. `[\"agent:read\", \"agent:write\"]`) |\n| `allowed_project_ids` | Permitted project UUID boundaries | List of UUIDs or None | None (All accessible) | Limits workload token authority strictly to designated project IDs |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for token exchange | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Restricts token exchange to specific runner network IP blocks |\n| `capability_mask` | Capability bitmask ceiling | Integer between 0 and 255 | 255 | Monotonic capability ceiling enforced by RLS |\n| `max_ttl_seconds` | Lifespan of issued ephemeral token | Integer between 60 and 86400 seconds | 3600 (1 hour) | Limits exposure window of federated workload credentials |\n| `is_enabled` | Policy active toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled policies immediately reject token exchange |\n\n- **Setting-by-Setting Workload Technical Details:**\n  - **`name`:** Descriptive name assigned to the workload identity policy (1 to 120 characters). Displayed in governance tables and audit records.\n  - **`description`:** Optional documentation field (up to 500 characters) explaining the pipeline context, team owner, or workflow triggering the token exchange.\n  - **`issuer`:** The trusted OpenID Connect issuer URL. Must use a strict HTTPS scheme (e.g. `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`). Plaintext HTTP URLs are rejected.\n  - **`client_id`:** Expected audience identifier (`aud`) matching the workload runner configuration (1 to 255 characters).\n  - **`subject_pattern`:** Glob pattern or regex matched against the incoming OIDC JWT `sub` claim (1 to 500 characters). Evaluated using POSIX pattern matching.\n  - **`required_claims_json`:** Arbitrary JSON dictionary of required key-value claims that must match exactly in the incoming token (e.g. branch, environment, or repository slug).\n  - **`surface`:** The target MCP surface granted to the federated token (`agent` or `docs`).\n  - **`scopes`:** List of allowed scopes granted upon successful exchange, restricted strictly to valid surface scopes (such as `agent:read`, `agent:write`, or `docs:read`).\n  - **`allowed_project_ids`:** Optional list of project UUIDs restricting where the exchanged token can query or mutate work items.\n  - **`allowed_cidrs`:** Optional network boundary allowlist restricting token exchange requests to authorized runner subnet IP addresses.\n  - **`capability_mask`:** Monotonic capability bitmask ceiling (0 to 255, default 255). Further constrains the operations permitted by the resulting token.\n  - **`max_ttl_seconds`:** Maximum validity period for issued short-lived access tokens (60 to 86,400 seconds, default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour).\n  - **`is_enabled`:** Administrative master switch for the policy. When set to `false`, token exchange requests matching this policy are rejected immediately.\n- **Built-in Presets:** The Studio provides one-click presets for common automated environments:\n  - **GitHub Actions:** Pre-fills issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com` and pattern `repo:org/repo:ref:refs/heads/*`.\n  - **GitLab CI:** Pre-fills issuer `https://gitlab.com` and pattern `project_path:group/project:ref:*:ref_type:branch`.\n  - **Kubernetes:** Pre-fills in-cluster issuer `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local` and pattern `system:serviceaccount:default:*`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, configures token lifetimes, and manages client revocation.\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Bulk Purge](images/slice3-oauth-governance-bulk-purge.png)\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `client_type` | Client confidentiality tier | `public`, `confidential` | `public` | Confidential clients require client secret authentication |\n| `auth_method` | Client authentication method | `none`, `client_secret_post`, `client_secret_basic`, `private_key_jwt` | `none` for public | Governs how client verifies identity at token endpoint |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforce` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `true` (`S256`) | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent:read`, `agent:write`, `docs:read`, `docs:write`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, `admin:destructive` | `agent:read` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (60 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour, 900 for JWT) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n| `max_state_len` | Maximum length of OAuth state parameter | 4096 characters | 4096 | Supports large serialized state payloads required by Google Gemini |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for client lifecycle view | `all`, `active`, `suspended`, `revoked` | `all` | Isolates active integrations from suspended or revoked clients |\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Paginated 500 Clients](images/slice3-oauth-governance-paginated-500.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`client_name`:** Human-readable title of the client application (1 to 128 characters). Displayed prominently to users on authorization consent screens and within the administrative governance directory.\n- **`client_id`:** Unique identifier generated upon client registration, prefixed with `client_` (`client_{secrets.token_urlsafe(16)}`). Serves as the public identifier across OAuth 2.1 authorization and device authorization flows.\n- **`client_type`:** Distinguishes between `public` clients (single-page applications, native desktop tools like Cursor or Claude Desktop, and CLI binaries that cannot safeguard secrets) and `confidential` clients (backend server applications capable of storing secrets securely).\n- **`auth_method`:** Defines the client authentication mechanism used at `/oauth/token`. Public clients use `none` with mandatory PKCE `S256`. Confidential clients use `client_secret_post` (secret in POST body), `client_secret_basic` (HTTP Basic authentication header), or `private_key_jwt` (RFC 7523 asymmetric client assertion).\n- **`redirect_uris`:** Exact list of authorized redirect callback URIs. Every entry must be a valid HTTPS URL, with `http://localhost` and `http://127.0.0.1` permitted exclusively for local development and desktop tools. Wildcards and open redirect patterns are strictly rejected.\n- **`pkce_enforce`:** Enforces Proof Key for Code Exchange (RFC 7636). \ud83d\udc4dkanban requires `code_challenge_method = S256` for all authorization code flows. Plain code challenges (`code_challenge_method = plain`) are rejected with `400 invalid_request`.\n- **`allowed_scopes`:** Space- or comma-delimited list of privilege scopes available to the client, strictly adhering to `SURFACE_SCOPES`: `agent:read`, `agent:write`, `docs:read`, `docs:write`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, and `admin:destructive`.\n- **`access_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of minted access tokens (configurable from 60 to 86,400 seconds; default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour for client credentials, 900 seconds / 15 minutes for JWT assertions). Minimizes the window of exposure if a bearer token is intercepted.\n- **`refresh_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of refresh tokens (86,400 to 2,592,000 seconds, default 30 days). Refresh tokens rotate upon every exchange; if a previously used refresh token is presented, the entire token family is revoked to prevent replay attacks.\n- **`max_state_len`:** Defines the maximum permitted length of the OAuth `state` parameter (4,096 characters). Specifically engineered to accommodate complex serialized routing and nonce payloads generated by Google Gemini and enterprise identity gateways. Requests exceeding 4,096 characters are rejected with HTTP 400.\n- **`status_filter`:** Filters displayed clients in the Governance Studio across four lifecycle states: `all` (complete registry), `active` (operational clients), `suspended` (temporarily blocked from token exchange), and `revoked` (permanently disabled).\n\n![Device Activation Page](images/slice3-device-activate-page.png)\n\n### RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Flow and Bulk Purge\n\n- **RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Authorization Flow:** For terminal agents and CLI tools unable to host local redirect servers, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements RFC 8628 device authorization:\n  - **Initiation:** The CLI calls `POST /oauth/device/authorize` with `client_id` and target `resource` (e.g. `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`).\n  - **User Code Formatting:** The server issues an 8-character `user_code` formatted in hyphenated Base20 (`XXXX-XXXX`) using charset `BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ` (excluding vowels `A,E,I,O,U` to avoid offensive words and numbers `0-9` to eliminate visual ambiguity).\n  - **Activation Interface:** The developer visits `/activate`, enters the code, reviews the requested resource and scopes, selects permitted projects, satisfies AAL2 step-up if administrative scopes are requested, and approves the session.\n  - **Token Exchange:** The CLI polls `POST /oauth/token` with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code` at the configured interval (default 5s) until approval completes, receiving an ephemeral scoped MCP access token.\n- **Single-Transaction Bulk DCR Client Purge:** High-volume test suites and dynamic client registration can create hundreds of orphaned client rows. The Governance Studio includes a **Purge Stale Clients** action (`POST /api/v1/admin/oauth-clients/bulk-delete`). The backend identifies all public DCR clients with zero active grants and zero refresh tokens, deleting up to thousands of stale records in a single atomic database transaction while preserving active production integrations intact.\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai` or `/workspaces/:ws/settings`) configures multi-provider LLM integrations, write-only envelope encryption for API credentials, failover priority ordering, and monthly token budgets with automated circuit breakers.\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-ai-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![AI Provider Editor Drawer - 0 Password Inputs](images/slice2-ai-provider-drawer-create.png)\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_kind` | Underlying AI runtime architecture | `gemini`, `openai`, `ollama` | Required | Governs protocol serialization, tool calling format, and streaming handlers |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable provider label | UTF-8 String (1 to 80 characters) | Required | Displayed on provider cards, failover stack, and token ledger |\n| `base_url` | Upstream API endpoint address | Valid HTTP or HTTPS URL | Provider default | Local Ollama uses `http://127.0.0.1:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Secret cryptographic provider credential | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; zero password inputs in client DOM |\n| `model_allowlist` | Permitted model identifiers for dispatch | Array or comma-separated list of strings | Probed list | Only allowlisted models are accessible for completion and agent execution |\n| `timeout_ms` | Hard ceiling on provider HTTP transactions | Integer between 1000 and 300000 ms | 30000 ms | Upstream requests exceeding timeout fail closed with `GATE_TIMEOUT` (max 300s) |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model sampling | Float between 0.0 and 2.0 | 0.7 | Low values (`0.1` to `0.3`) ensure deterministic verification gate verdicts |\n| `top_p` | Nucleus sampling probability cutoff | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 1.0 | Filters tail tokens; 1.0 evaluates the full cumulative distribution |\n| `max_tokens` | Maximum generation token ceiling | Integer between 1 and 128000 tokens | 4096 | Constrains generation size to avoid runaway completion loops |\n| `mtls_client_cert_pem` | Client certificate for mutual TLS | Valid PEM certificate block or empty | None | Enables certificate-authenticated transport to private gateways |\n| `is_enabled` | Master active toggle for the provider | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled providers are skipped during model probing and runtime dispatch |\n| `period_kind` | Accounting duration for consumption caps | `daily` or `monthly` | `monthly` | Accounting period tier: daily or monthly (resets token consumption counter) |\n| `token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget limit | Non-negative integer (0 to 10000000000) | 10000000000 | `0` enforces Hard AI Freeze; `10000000000` enables Unlimited Quota |\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Unlimited Quota](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-unlimited.png)\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Hard AI Freeze Warning](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-freeze.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: AI Providers and Token Budgets\n\n- **provider_kind:** Identifies the foundational AI provider protocol engine. Supported choices are `gemini` (Google Gemini 2.5 Flash/Pro with native function calling and multimodal spec support), `openai` (OpenAI GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, and compatible gateways implementing standard JSON schema tool definitions), and `ollama` (local air-gapped runtimes for private edge models like Llama 3 and DeepSeek-R1). Selecting a kind binds the request serializer, streaming parser, and error mapper.\n- **display_name:** The descriptive title assigned to the provider instance (for example, \"Google Gemini Workspace\" or \"Local Ollama Runtime\"). Scoped to the workspace or server tier, this label identifies the provider across cards in `/admin/ai`, the failover priority stack, and the audit ledger.\n- **base_url:** The root network URL for provider API communications. Cloud providers require secure HTTPS endpoints (`https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com` or `https://api.openai.com/v1`), whereas local Ollama deployments typically connect over internal loopback (`http://127.0.0.1:11434`). Outbound calls are subject to network policy and workspace air-gap restrictions.\n- **api_key:** The secret API key used to authenticate upstream cloud requests. Submitted over TLS, stored encrypted at rest via PostgreSQL envelope encryption, and never echoed back to client browsers. In `AiProviderEditorDrawer`, input fields adhere to strict security invariants by using standard masked inputs with 0 `input[type=\"password\"]` elements, preventing browser credential managers from storing or sniffing AI keys.\n- **model_allowlist:** An explicit allowlist of model names permitted for execution under this provider configuration (e.g. `[\"gemini-2.5-flash\", \"gemini-2.5-pro\"]` or `[\"llama3.2:latest\"]`). Administrators can manually specify identifiers or click `Probe Models` to automatically query the upstream provider API and select discovered models via interactive search chips.\n- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock request duration allowed for provider calls, bounded between 1,000 ms and 300,000 ms (default 30,000 ms, with a 300-second reasoning ceiling). When combined with agent execution, the runtime enforces `min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. If the upstream model exceeds this cutoff, the system aborts execution, fails closed, and records `GATE_TIMEOUT` without hanging worker threads.\n- **temperature:** Governs generation randomness on a scale from 0.0 to 2.0 (default 0.7). Lower temperatures (0.1 to 0.3) provide highly deterministic outputs recommended for code review, transition gates, and structured JSON parsing, while moderate values (0.7 to 1.0) support creative sticky synthesis and brainstorming.\n- **top_p:** Controls nucleus sampling by restricting generation to tokens comprising the top P probability mass (0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0). Decreasing this value eliminates low-probability generation branches, enhancing predictability in mission-critical QA workflows.\n- **max_tokens:** The upper bound on token output generated in a single completion or reasoning turn (configurable from 1 to 128,000 tokens, default 4,096). Protects against unbounded model loops and limits context window consumption.\n- **mtls_client_cert_pem:** An optional client certificate in PEM format used when establishing mutual TLS connections to enterprise AI gateways or private proxy infrastructure.\n- **is_enabled:** A boolean toggle that immediately activates or deactivates the provider without deleting its configuration or keys. Inactive providers are bypassed during live completion dispatch and sparkle routing.\n- **priority_and_order:** The failover dispatch sequence determined via `PUT /api/v1/admin/ai/providers/order` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/order`). Providers are evaluated sequentially by priority. If a higher-tier provider encounters a 5xx error or rate limit, the router seamlessly falls back to the next active provider in the stack.\n- **token_cap_and_budgets:** Managed via `PUT /api/v1/admin/ai/budgets` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/budgets`) across daily or monthly periods (`period_kind`). Setting `token_cap = 0` triggers a Hard AI Freeze, immediately rejecting reservations with `BudgetExceeded` and halting Sparkle completions with status `budget`. Selecting Unlimited Quota configures an internal ceiling of 10,000,000,000 tokens, recording ledger telemetry without automated cutoffs. Quick presets provide standard allowances of 1M, 5M, 25M, and 100M tokens.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents` or `/projects/:proj/settings/agents`) configures autonomous agent personas, tool permission boundaries, execution harnesses, and developer onboarding prompts.\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `slug` | Unique URL-safe agent identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (1 to 64 chars) | Required | Used in ticket `@mentions`, API URLs, and activity logs |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable name of the agent | UTF-8 String (1 to 200 characters) | Required | Displayed on ticket assignment cards and inspection drawers |\n| `harness_kind` | Runtime execution environment | `in_app_sdk`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `in_app_sdk` | Governs execution boundary, isolation model, and process privileges |\n| `provider_kind` | AI provider bound to the agent | `gemini`, `openai`, `ollama` | Provider default | Used when `harness_kind = in_app_sdk` to route LLM queries |\n| `model_id` | Model slug for agent reasoning | Valid model identifier string | Provider default | Controls reasoning depth and token processing speed |\n| `cli_binary_allowlist_id`| Pre-approved binary for CLI pipe harness | `agy`, `codex` | `agy` | Zero-shell execution over stdin/stdout inside hardened Linux jail |\n| `cli_args` | Command-line arguments for CLI pipe | Array of string arguments | `[\"-p\"]` | Structured arguments passed directly via execve (never shell) |\n| `remote_runner_pool_slug`| Target pool for remote worker dispatch | Valid pool slug identifier | `default` | Routes heavy build tasks to distributed mTLS worker nodes |\n| `repo_access_mode` | Repository filesystem access permissions | `none`, `ro_vfs_mount`, `bare_git_tools` | `none` | `ro_vfs_mount` mounts Git repo read-only; prevents code tampering |\n| `mcp_allowed_tools` | Permitted Model Context Protocol tools | Array of tool names | Basic read set | Restricts capabilities to least privilege allowlist (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`) |\n| `system_instructions_md`| Persona prompt and operational charter | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown | Required | Directs reasoning behavior and verification requirements |\n| `timeout_ms` | Maximum execution time before cancellation | Integer between 1000 and 300000 ms | 60000 ms | Hard cutoff ceiling; uncompleted tasks fail closed safely (max 300s) |\n| `project_id` | Project scoping and tenancy binding | Valid UUID or null | Null (Workspace) | Enforces PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` tenant isolation |\n| `agent_onboarding_prefs`| User preferences for agent onboarding | JSON object | `{}` | Stores execution mode, model strategy, and QA rigor from `/llms.txt` |\n\n![Agent Validation Modal - 4-Section Trace](images/slice2-agent-validation-modal-trace.png)\n\n![Agent Onboarding Card - Desktop Light](images/slice2-onboarding-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: Agent Definitions and Onboarding\n\n- **slug:** The immutable machine identifier for the agent definition (e.g. `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`). Constrained to lowercase alphanumeric ASCII characters and hyphens. The slug forms the primary address for ticket comments, mention notifications, and REST endpoint routes (`/api/v1/admin/agent-definitions/:slug`).\n- **display_name:** The human-facing label rendered on board cards, ticket assignment selectors, peek drawers, and audit trails. Allows teams to provide intuitive persona titles (e.g. \"Forensic QA Reality Checker\" or \"Architecture Auditor\").\n- **harness_kind:** Specifies the runtime execution architecture. `in_app_sdk` executes in-process using configured cloud or local LLM providers. `cli_pipe` executes external command-line binaries (`agy`, `codex`) inside a zero-shell Linux namespace jail (`nsjail`), communicating strictly over standard input/output with 512 MB memory caps and zero network access. `remote_runner` dispatches tasks to distributed worker daemons connected over mutual TLS.\n- **provider_kind and model_id:** Applicable to `in_app_sdk` harnesses. Specifies the active provider backend (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`) and specific model (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`, `llama3.2:latest`) utilized for the agent persona's completion and tool-calling cycles.\n- **cli_binary_allowlist_id and cli_args:** Governs `cli_pipe` execution. Restricts binary invocations to pre-vetted host binaries (`agy`, `codex`). Arguments are submitted as an array of safe flags (default `[\"-p\"]`) executed directly via `execve`, eliminating shell injection hazards (`/bin/sh` is never spawned).\n- **remote_runner_pool_slug:** Designates the distributed worker pool (such as `default` or `gpu-compute`) responsible for accepting tasks when `harness_kind = remote_runner`.\n- **repo_access_mode:** Enforces Git repository access boundaries during task execution. In `none` mode, the agent operates solely on ticket prose. In `ro_vfs_mount` mode, the target repository is mounted strictly read-only (`CLONE_NEWNS`), allowing static code analysis while preventing unauthorized workspace writes. In `bare_git_tools` mode, read-only bare Git inspection tools are provisioned in an isolated tmpfs directory.\n- **mcp_allowed_tools:** An array of tool names defining the explicit Model Context Protocol allowlist for the agent. Tools outside this list (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`) are hidden from the agent's schema, enforcing strict privilege separation (submitting unlisted tools fails with HTTP 422 `HARNESS_FIELDS_INVALID`).\n- **system_instructions_md:** The core persona prompt formatted in canonical UTF-8 Markdown. Directs agent analysis, defines verification standards, and specifies output formats. Server-side sanitization strips lone surrogates and harmful prompt injection vectors before database storage.\n- **timeout_ms:** Maximum allowable execution time for agent invocations, configurable from 1,000 ms to 300,000 ms (default 60,000 ms, adhering to the 300-second reasoning ceiling from KAN-430). Tasks that exceed this threshold are terminated immediately, preserving thread pools and avoiding stuck workflows.\n- **project_id and rls_isolation:** Binds the agent definition to a specific project UUID or leaves it at workspace scope. PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` strictly isolates project agents: administrators and members of other projects cannot view, mutate, or validate cross-project agents (returning HTTP 404 Not Found).\n- **agent_validation_modal:** Invoked via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-definitions/:id:validate` or `POST /api/v1/agent-definitions/:id:validate`. Executes a live non-destructive test invocation returning a 4-section forensic verification report: 1. `=== 1. RENDERED CONTEXT ===`, 2. `=== 2. TOOL TRACE ===`, 3. `=== 3. LATENCY & TOKENS ===`, and 4. `=== 4. SCHEMA & POLICY STATUS ===`.\n- **llms_txt_and_onboarding_card:** Machine-readable agent capabilities and skill manifests served at `/llms.txt`. The onboarding card surfaces the canonical onboarding prompt that guides external agents (Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI) through an interactive setup interview, persisting user preferences (`execution_mode`, `model_strategy`, `qa_rigor`, `custom_notes`) to `agent_onboarding_prefs` in `/api/v1/users/me/preferences` strictly within the local project folder without polluting global system paths.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) monitors distributed worker nodes, mints single-use enrollment tokens, and manages hardware-bound mutual TLS (mTLS) identities.\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `hostname` | Unique network hostname of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health cards and active execution telemetry |\n| `pool_slug` | Resource pool grouping for runner fleet | Lowercase ASCII slug | `default` | Allows target assignment of jobs to specialized hardware fleets |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic enrollment token | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires in 60s to 900s (max 15 min); burns upon first enrollment |\n| `client_cert_sha256_hex`| SHA-256 fingerprint of client certificate | 64-char hexadecimal string | Computed at enroll | Binds hardware identity to mTLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `cert_expires_at` | Client certificate expiration timestamp | ISO-8601 Timestamp | 30 days from mint | Enforces periodic hardware credential rotation |\n| `status` | Current operational connectivity state | `online`, `offline`, `revoked` | `offline` | Only `online` runners receive dispatched build and verification jobs |\n| `last_heartbeat_at` | Timestamp of most recent daemon ping | ISO-8601 Timestamp or null | Null | Heartbeat frame received every 15s; missing 45s marks runner offline (`DEAD_EVICTION_S = 45.0`) |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Canonical enrollment CLI invocation | Shell command string | Auto-generated | `echo \"$TOKEN\" | uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `state_dir_permissions` | POSIX permissions on runner daemon host | Octal mode (`0o700`, `0o600`, `0o644`) | Strict defaults | `0o700` state dir, `0o600` `client.key` & `ca.pem`, `0o644` `client.crt` |\n| `mtls_pop_challenge` | Challenge format for Proof-of-Possession | Text challenge string | Auto-generated | Syntax: `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}` |\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal](images/slice2-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: Remote Runners Fleet\n\n- **hostname and pool_slug:** The runner hostname uniquely identifies the physical or virtual execution host (for example, `worker-01.internal.test`). The pool slug groups runners into specialized task fleets (such as `default` or `gpu-compute`), enabling workflows to direct heavy verification suites to properly provisioned worker infrastructure.\n- **enrollment_token:** Ephemeral, single-use authentication token minted via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/enrollment-tokens`. Token lifetime is strictly capped between 60 and 900 seconds (maximum 15 minutes). The token is consumed immediately upon successful certificate signing; replaying an expired or consumed token is rejected with HTTP 401 `ENROLLMENT_TOKEN_INVALID`.\n- **client_cert_sha256_hex:** The cryptographic SHA-256 digest of the X.509 client certificate generated during runner daemon enrollment. The orchestrator registers this fingerprint in the database, verifying that incoming mutual TLS connections match the approved hardware certificate.\n- **cert_expires_at:** The expiration timestamp for the signed client certificate (standard 30-day validity). When a certificate approaches expiration, the runner must be re-enrolled using a freshly minted token.\n- **status and heartbeats:** Reflects runner operational status (`online`, `offline`, or `revoked`). Active daemons send periodic `HeartbeatFrame` packets every 15 seconds over the established mTLS WebSocket channel. If no heartbeat is received within 45 seconds (`DEAD_EVICTION_S = 45.0`), the runner transitions to `offline`, and pending tasks are rescheduled.\n- **unprivileged_daemon_and_posix_permissions:** The runner daemon (`kanban-agent-runner`) executes as an unprivileged, non-root system account. Local cryptographic assets are created with strict POSIX file permissions: state directory `0o700` (`drwx------`), private key `client.key` `0o600` (`-rw-------`), root CA bundle `ca.pem` `0o600` (`-rw-------`), and client certificate `client.crt` `0o644` (`-rw-r--r--`).\n- **mtls_pop_handshake:** Mutual TLS connections connect to dedicated port `:8444` (or WebSocket route `/api/v1/agent-runners/ws`). During handshake, the orchestrator issues a `ChallengeHelloFrame` containing an ephemeral nonce. The runner signs the canonical challenge payload `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}` using its private EC key, proving certificate possession before transitioning to `online`.\n- **runner_revocation:** Administrators can instantly revoke rogue or decommissioned runners via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/:id:revoke`. Revocation immediately terminates active WebSocket connections and flags the certificate fingerprint as revoked. Subsequent connection attempts are rejected with HTTP 403 Forbidden.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed in place. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
+    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspace-settings`) manages top-level tenant policies, display metadata, session lifetimes, trash retention, export permissions, and air-gap network boundaries under strict AAL2 authentication.\n\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Light](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workspace Settings General - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workspace-settings-general-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Workspace Settings Nested Groups](images/slice5-workspace-settings-groups-nested-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Workspace Settings Trash Retention](images/slice5-workspace-settings-trash-retention-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Canonical internal name of the workspace | UTF-8 String (1 to 200 characters) | Required at provisioning | Tenant root identifier in PostgreSQL RLS context |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace slug used in REST routing and browser paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 63 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable tenant boundary in database session context |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable workspace title displayed in navigation header | UTF-8 String (1 to 80 characters) | `\"Workspace\"` | Visible across topbar, breadcrumbs, and notification emails |\n| `default_project_visibility` | Baseline privacy level assigned to newly initialized projects | `\"private\"`, `\"workspace\"` | `\"private\"` | Prevents accidental cross-project disclosure in multi-team workspaces |\n| `session_idle_timeout_min` | Inactivity window before user session transitions to stale | Integer between 5 and 720 minutes | 60 | Enforces local re-authentication after inactivity period |\n| `session_absolute_timeout_min` | Maximum lifetime of an authentication session cookie | Integer between 60 and 10080 minutes | 720 (12h) | Requires full re-login upon absolute expiration |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Duration soft-deleted items remain in Trash before permanent purge | Integer between 7 and 365 days | 30 | Governs compliance data retention and recovery windows |\n| `mcp_key_max_ttl_days` | Maximum validity period permitted for minted MCP API keys | Integer between 1 and 365 days | 90 | Caps autonomous agent credential lifetimes |\n| `air_gap_mode` | Strict isolation mode blocking external cloud AI and external egress | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When active, restricts AI execution strictly to local Ollama and on-prem runners |\n| `default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers` | Agent-Safe Sequence Numbering policy toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Skips culturally and technically loaded sequence numbers (`13`, `69`, `88`, `404`, `420`, `666`, `1337`, `1488`) during issue sequence allocation |\n| `issue_export_enabled` | Allows exporting work items to JSON or CSV archives | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Controlled data exfiltration prevention |\n| `issue_export_allowed_roles` | Explicit RBAC roles permitted to export work item data | List of role strings (`\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, `\"member\"`, `\"guest\"`) | `[\"workspace_admin\", \"project_admin\", \"member\"]` | Restricts bulk export capability to authorized team members |\n| `issue_import_enabled` | Allows lossless ingestion from external trackers | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Gates administrative bulk ingestion workflows |\n| `report_export_enabled` | Allows exporting statistical flow reports and audit logs | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Governs access to compliance and telemetry exports |\n| `report_export_allowed_roles` | Roles allowed to download statistical flow telemetry | List of role strings (`\"ADMIN\"`, `\"MEMBER\"`) | Both roles | Prevents unauthorized aggregation of engineering velocity data |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workspace Settings)\n\n- **name:** Internal database name representing the workspace tenant in PostgreSQL. Required during bootstrap and immutable across standard operation, binding the tenant scope for Row-Level Security policies.\n- **slug:** URL-safe ASCII slug identifying the tenant in REST API endpoints (`/api/v1/workspaces/{slug}`) and browser URL routes (`/{slug}/projects`). Must be between 2 and 63 alphanumeric characters and hyphens.\n- **display_name:** The human-facing label rendered on topbars, page document titles, mobile shells, and notification cards. Supports UTF-8 strings up to 80 characters with full internationalization.\n- **default_project_visibility:** Sets the default privacy tier assigned when users create new projects. Options are `\"private\"` (accessible strictly to explicitly invited members and project administrators, default) or `\"workspace\"` (visible to all authenticated workspace members).\n- **session_idle_timeout_min:** Controls how long an idle user session remains active before requiring step-up authentication or PIN challenge. Configurable from 5 minutes to 720 minutes (12 hours), with a standard enterprise default of 60 minutes.\n- **session_absolute_timeout_min:** Hard maximum duration for any active session cookie regardless of user activity. Configurable between 60 minutes (1 hour) and 10080 minutes (7 days), defaulting to 720 minutes (12 hours).\n- **trash_retention_days:** Defines how many days soft-deleted work items, docs, stickies, and cycles reside in the Trash Studio (`/trash`) before being permanently purged by the background vacuum worker. Values range from 7 to 365 days (default 30 days).\n- **mcp_key_max_ttl_days:** Upper boundary enforced when administrators or developers mint Model Context Protocol (MCP) scoped tokens. The backend rejects any key creation request specifying a TTL exceeding this configured cap (1 to 365 days, default 90 days).\n- **air_gap_mode:** When set to `true`, \ud83d\udc4dkanban activates zero-external-egress enforcement. All outbound requests to cloud AI providers (OpenAI, Gemini) and remote webhooks are fail-closed blocked at the transport layer, restricting agent pipelines exclusively to LAN Ollama servers and local CLI runners.\n- **default_skip_loaded_sequence_numbers:** An Agent-Safe Sequence Numbering policy flag. When enabled, the sequence allocator automatically skips culturally and technically loaded issue sequence numbers (`13`, `69`, `88`, `404`, `420`, `666`, `1337`, `1488`) during work item creation (DB/API default `false`).\n- **issue_export_enabled:** Master toggle for work item data extraction. Disabling this flag blocks `/api/v1/projects/{project}/issues/export` across both the REST API and the web interface to prevent bulk data exfiltration.\n- **issue_export_allowed_roles:** Role allowlist determining who can trigger issue exports. Configurable with combinations of `\"workspace_admin\"`, `\"project_admin\"`, `\"member\"`, and `\"guest\"`, defaulting to `[\"workspace_admin\", \"project_admin\", \"member\"]` (3 roles, excluding guest accounts).\n- **issue_import_enabled:** Administrative toggle governing data ingestion from external platforms (Plane, Jira, GitHub, Linear). Disabling this blocks `/api/v1/admin/imports` to preserve data integrity during audit periods.\n- **report_export_enabled:** Controls whether statistical flow telemetry, Monte Carlo lead-time forecasts, and velocity percentiles can be downloaded as CSV or JSON payloads.\n- **report_export_allowed_roles:** Specifies the minimum permission tier required to export engineering flow reports (options include `\"ADMIN\"` and `\"MEMBER\"`).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Configuring Workspace Policies and Trash Retention\n\n1. **Access Workspace Settings:** Click your avatar or the settings cog in the top navigation bar and select **Workspace Settings** (or navigate directly to `/workspaces/<slug>/settings`).\n2. **Review General Policies:** In the General tab, update your Display Name, verify the Workspace Slug, and select the Default Project Visibility (`Private` or `Workspace`).\n3. **Configure Session Security:** Under Session Lifetimes, configure Session Idle Timeout (e.g. 60 minutes) to force re-authentication for unattended terminals, and set Session Absolute Timeout (e.g. 720 minutes / 12 hours) to mandate periodic credential rotation.\n4. **Tune Compliance & Trash Retention:** In the Data Lifecycle & Trash card, adjust Trash Retention Days between 7 and 365 days (default 30 days). Soft-deleted work items remain recoverable in Trash for this duration before automated irreversible purging.\n5. **Manage Air-Gap and Export Restrictions:** If operating in a sovereign or disconnected environment, toggle Air-Gap Mode to ON to restrict agent and runner execution to LAN hosts. Review Allowed Export Roles to govern bulk data extraction.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **Save Settings** to persist the configuration to the database and audit ledger. Changes take effect across the workspace immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio & Personal Settings\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project workflows, custom status states, WIP limits, priority emojis, labels, and two-stage project deletion. Personal Settings (`/settings` or `/profile`) manages user profiles, canonical timezones, custom avatars, password rotation, Passkeys, TOTP 2FA, recovery codes, and external identity linking.\n\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Priorities - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-priorities-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings WIP Limits - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-wip-limits-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Light](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Project Settings Danger Zone - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-project-settings-danger-zone-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable project display name | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in topbars and command palette |\n| `key` | Issue identifier prefix (e.g. `KAN`, `CORE`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]{1,9}$` (2 to 10 chars) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates unique issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `state_name` | Name of workflow state (e.g. `In Progress`) | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on board columns, list groupings, and peek drawers |\n| `group` | Lifecycle category mapping | `\"backlog\"`, `\"unstarted\"`, `\"started\"`, `\"completed\"`, `\"cancelled\"` | Required | Drives cycle velocity metrics, burn-downs, and SLA calculations |\n| `color_hex` | Hexadecimal color accent for state badge | 7-character hex code (`^#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}$`) | `#3B82F6` | Provides distinct visual identification across board and table views |\n| `sort_order` | Execution ordering of states across the board | Integer (0 to 1000) | `0` | Controls horizontal board lane arrangement and transition flow |\n| `is_default` | Default state assigned to newly created issues | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Exactly one state per project can be designated default |\n| `wip_soft_limit` | Warning threshold for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Renders subtle amber capacity warnings when column count is exceeded |\n| `wip_hard_limit` | Strict blocking limit for work-in-progress cards | Integer between 1 and 999 (or `null`) | `null` (None) | Disallows moving or creating cards into lane when limit is reached |\n| `priority_slug` | Normalized priority key (e.g. `urgent`, `high`) | Lowercase regex `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | Required | Standardized priority value used in query filters and formula AST |\n| `priority_label` | Human-readable priority label with emoji | UTF-8 String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed on card badges, priority dropdowns, and peek rails |\n| `exclusive_group` | Mutual exclusion category for label grouping | Lowercase regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` | `null` (Optional) | Enforces single-selection among conflicting tags (e.g. `env:prod`) |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Project Settings)\n\n- **name:** The project name displayed across navigation breadcrumbs, issue peek drawers, and workspace summary cards. Scoped strictly to the active workspace (max 120 characters).\n- **key:** The immutable uppercase prefix key identifying work items within this project (e.g. `KAN` generates `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). Validated against `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]{1,9}$` (between 2 and 10 alphanumeric characters).\n- **state_name:** The human-facing title of a workflow state (e.g. `Backlog`, `In Progress`, `Code Review`, `Done`). Renaming a state immediately reflects across all active cards without modifying historical audit events.\n- **group:** The canonical lifecycle category to which the state belongs (`backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled`). Group mapping is fundamental: cycle burndowns, cumulative flow diagrams, and throughput percentiles aggregate work based on group identity.\n- **color_hex:** A 7-character hexadecimal color string (`#RRGGBB`) applied to column lane headers, state pills, and board drop zones to ensure high contrast and immediate readability.\n- **sort_order:** An integer specifying the left-to-right presentation order of board columns and workflow dropdowns. Lower numbers appear earlier in the delivery lifecycle.\n- **is_default:** Designates the initial workflow state automatically assigned when a contributor quick-creates an issue without specifying a state. Exactly one state per project can be default.\n- **wip_soft_limit:** An advisory Work-In-Progress limit for Kanban columns (1 to 999). When the active card count exceeds this number, the column header displays an amber count badge (`5/4 WIP`) without preventing drops.\n- **wip_hard_limit:** A strict Work-In-Progress enforcement threshold (1 to 999). When reached, further card drops or status changes targeting this column are rejected at the API boundary (`422 WIP_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`). Must be greater than or equal to `wip_soft_limit`.\n- **priority_slug:** Machine-readable identifier for priority tiers (`urgent`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `none`). Used in Safe Formula evaluations, MCP tool queries, and REST search filters.\n- **priority_label:** The user-facing priority string, typically combining an expressive semantic emoji and clear text (e.g. `\ud83d\udd34 Urgent`, `\ud83d\udfe0 High`, `\ud83d\udfe1 Medium`, `\ud83d\udd35 Low`).\n- **exclusive_group:** An optional label category constraint. When applied to tags (e.g. `tier:free`, `tier:pro`), applying one tag from the group automatically removes any previously attached tags in that same exclusive group.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Setting Project WIP Limits and Workflow States\n\n1. **Navigate to Project Settings:** Open your target project (e.g. `/projects/KAN`) and click **Settings** in the project sidebar.\n2. **Open Workflow States Studio:** Select the **States** tab to view all currently configured workflow states grouped by lifecycle phase.\n3. **Add or Edit a State:** Click **+ Add State** or click the edit icon on an existing state row. Specify the State Name (e.g. `In Review`), assign the Lifecycle Group (`started` for active work, `completed` for resolved work), choose an Accent Color, and check Make Default if newly created tickets should land in this column.\n4. **Configure WIP Limits:** Enter a Soft WIP Limit (e.g. `4`) to flag column congestion, and enter a Hard WIP Limit (e.g. `6`) if your team enforces strict pull-based flow and prohibits card overloading (`wip_soft_limit <= wip_hard_limit`).\n5. **Reorder Board Columns:** Drag states up and down using the grip handle to adjust their execution sort order from left to right.\n6. **Save Changes:** Click **Save States** to persist the configuration. Board columns update immediately across all connected WebSockets.\n\n---\n\n### Personal Settings\nThe Personal Settings Studio (`/settings` or `/profile`) provides comprehensive user identity self-service, including canonical timezone localization, avatar upload with automatic sanitization and cropping, password rotation with server-admin entropy rules, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP two-factor authentication, one-time emergency recovery codes, and external OIDC/LDAP identity account linking.\n\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Light](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Personal Settings - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-personal-settings-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Personal Settings - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Personal Settings - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-personal-settings-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Personal Settings Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `display_name` | Public user profile name displayed to colleagues | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | User's full name | Sanitized against invisible Unicode characters and control codes |\n| `timezone` | Canonical IANA timezone used for date formatting | 15 canonical timezones (e.g. `America/New_York`, `UTC`) | `UTC` | Localizes ticket creation, audit timestamps, and SLA due date displays |\n| `avatar` | User profile photograph or custom avatar | PNG, JPEG, or WebP image file (<= 2 MiB) | Default letter icon | Re-encoded server-side via Pillow; SVG uploads rejected fail-closed |\n| `current_password` | Verification of existing credentials before password update | String matching current password | Required on change | Prevents unauthorized password changes on unattended sessions |\n| `new_password` | Updated local authentication password | Min 12 chars (Min 14 for Server Admins) | User-supplied | Salted and hashed via Argon2id with server-side pepper |\n| `passkeys` | FIDO2 / WebAuthn biometric security keys | Platform (Touch ID/Hello) or Cross-Platform (YubiKey) | None enrolled | Hardware-bound cryptographic public key authentication |\n| `totp_enabled` | Time-based one-time password (RFC 6238) 2FA | 6-digit verification code from authenticator app | Disabled | Requires AAL2 step-up challenge to disable once activated |\n| `recovery_codes` | Single-use emergency account recovery backup codes | 10 alphanumeric single-use codes | Generated on 2FA setup | Stored hashed in PostgreSQL; invalidated upon individual consumption |\n| `linked_identities` | External OIDC, Google, GitHub, or LDAP accounts | Verified external provider identity claims | Primary login card | Enables single-sign-on without password storage; unlinking requires AAL2 |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Personal Settings)\n\n- **display_name:** The user's public identity rendered across comments, avatar badges, activity feeds, and assignment menus. Cleaned server-side using Unicode guards to strip zero-width spaces and spoofing homoglyphs.\n- **timezone:** The user's localized timezone selection, chosen from 15 canonical IANA timezones (such as `America/New_York`, `Europe/London`, `Asia/Tokyo`, `UTC`). All timestamps remain stored canonically as UTC in PostgreSQL and are formatted to the user's local offset on the client.\n- **avatar:** Profile picture uploaded via `PUT /api/v1/auth/me/avatar`. The backend enforces a strict 2 MiB size cap and validates the raster payload via content-sniffing. Dangerous vector formats (SVG) are rejected fail-closed to eliminate XSS risks, and images are re-encoded to sanitized PNG format.\n- **current_password:** Required whenever a user modifies their local password via `POST /api/v1/auth/me/password`. Guards against session hijacking by requiring proof of current knowledge before modifying credentials.\n- **new_password:** The updated password. The platform enforces strict entropy requirements: standard users must supply at least 12 characters, while users possessing the `SERVER_ADMIN` role must supply at least 14 characters.\n- **passkeys:** WebAuthn credentials registered via `POST /api/v1/auth/webauthn/register:start` and `:finish`. Passkeys support both platform biometrics (Touch ID, Face ID, Windows Hello) and cross-platform hardware tokens (YubiKey). Each enrolled key displays nickname, enrolled date, and a non-intrusive **Test Passkey** verification button.\n- **totp_enabled:** Activates Time-Based One-Time Password verification for authenticator applications (Aegis, Google Authenticator, 1Password). Enrolled via QR code and verified with a 6-digit challenge code. Disabling TOTP requires mandatory AAL2 step-up verification.\n- **recovery_codes:** A set of 10 single-use emergency recovery codes generated when two-factor authentication is configured. Each code can be used exactly once to regain account access if hardware tokens or authenticator apps are lost.\n- **linked_identities:** Manages connected third-party enterprise identity cards (Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Okta, Active Directory LDAP). Users can link multiple external accounts to a single local identity or unlink secondary providers after completing AAL2 step-up verification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio & Git Repo Bindings\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) coordinates lifecycle rules, pre-transition validators, field mutators, asynchronous AI agent gates, and read-only bare Git repository mirror bindings.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-workflow-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-workflow-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive label for transition helper hook | UTF-8 String (1 to 120 characters) | Required | Displayed in pipeline lists, dry-run logs, and error explanations |\n| `from_state` | Origin workflow state of the transition rule | Valid state name or `null` (Any state) | `null` (Wildcard) | Restricts hook evaluation to specific card movements |\n| `to_state` | Destination workflow state of the transition | Valid project state name | Required | The target column being protected by the transition helper |\n| `hook_kind` | Execution engine and evaluation timing | `\"sync_validator\"`, `\"sync_mutator\"`, `\"agent_interceptor\"` | `\"sync_validator\"` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if helper times out or rejects | `\"fail_closed\"`, `\"fail_open_warn\"` | `\"fail_closed\"` | Fail-closed guarantees invalid transitions never commit |\n| `wasm_module_sha256` | SHA-256 digest of sandboxed Wasm module | 64-character lowercase hex string | Required for Wasm | Verifies bytecode authenticity before in-process execution |\n| `agent` | Identifier of registered autonomous agent | Valid agent slug (e.g. `reality-checker`) | Required for Agent | Binds asynchronous gate to configured LLM or CLI runner harness |\n| `timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed execution time in milliseconds | Integer between 1 and 300000 ms | 60000 for Agent | Protects transition queues from hanging subprocesses |\n| `allowed_mutable_fields` | Explicit field allowlist for mutator hooks | List of field names (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, `custom_fields.<slug>`) | `[]` (Empty) | Enforces least-privilege mutation; `state` and `description` are forbidden |\n| `repo_slug` | Identifier for project bare Git mirror | Regex `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,62}$` | Required | Names local bare mirror directory under `/var/lib/kanban/repos/` |\n| `remote_url` | Upstream Git repository clone URL | HTTPS or SSH Git URL | Required | Validated against SSRF policy before initiating background fetch |\n| `default_ref` | Primary branch or ref used for agent diff inspections | Valid Git ref name (e.g. `main`, `master`) | `\"main\"` | Baseline target branch used by reality-checker audits |\n| `access_mode` | Storage access mode for repository mirror | `\"ro_vfs_mount\"`, `\"bare_git_tools\"` | `\"ro_vfs_mount\"` | Enforces read-only filesystem or git tool execution |\n| `credential` | Optional credential reference for private repository authentication | `null`, `\"workspace-pat\"`, `\"ssh-key\"` | `null` | Secure token reference stored in credentials vault |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Workflow Transition Studio & Repo Bindings)\n\n- **name:** The user-defined identifier assigned to the workflow hook (such as `qa-done-gate` or `enforce-pr-link`, 1 to 120 characters). Appears on pipeline transition cards, dry-run evaluation outputs, and timeline rejection entries.\n- **from_state:** The initial state of the card before transition (e.g. `In Progress`). Selecting `null` creates a wildcard rule matching transitions originating from any state into `to_state`.\n- **to_state:** The protected destination state (e.g. `Done`). The card cannot land in this state until all attached synchronous validators pass and all asynchronous agent gates approve.\n- **hook_kind:** Defines the execution engine: `\"sync_validator\"` runs fast in-process WebAssembly checks (<25ms); `\"sync_mutator\"` applies deterministic property updates; `\"agent_interceptor\"` dispatches an asynchronous background gate to an AI model or test runner.\n- **failure_policy:** The error handling invariant. `\"fail_closed\"` (default) blocks the transition immediately if the helper fails, times out, or errors. `\"fail_open_warn\"` logs an administrative warning to the activity ledger and allows the card transition to complete.\n- **wasm_module_sha256:** The cryptographic hash of the compiled WebAssembly bytecode module executed by the `wasmtime` engine. Prevents execution of unverified or tampered binaries.\n- **agent:** The slug of the registered autonomous agent (`@reality-checker`, `@security-auditor`) assigned to evaluate asynchronous gate transitions. The attach dialog groups available agents by execution harness (`cli_pipe`, `in_app_sdk`, `remote_runner`).\n- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock execution deadline allocated to the helper (validating between 1 and 300,000 ms, defaulting to 60,000 ms for agent interceptors).\n- **allowed_mutable_fields:** A strict security allowlist restricting which issue properties a `sync_mutator` hook can modify (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, and `custom_fields.<slug>`). Project invariants strictly forbid modifying `state` or `description_markdown` to prevent recursive transition loops and content tampering.\n- **repo_slug:** The local filesystem folder name allocated to the bare Git mirror. Must be a clean lowercase alphanumeric string with hyphens.\n- **remote_url:** The external Git repository URL (e.g. `https://github.com/org/repo.git`). The server validates the URL against an anti-SSRF policy (blocking RFC 1918 private IP addresses unless explicitly allowlisted) before establishing outbound connections.\n- **default_ref:** The primary branch or ref name against which code inspection scripts, PR validation checks, and diff analyses are evaluated (default `\"main\"`).\n- **access_mode:** Defines how agents access repository contents: `\"ro_vfs_mount\"` mounts a read-only filesystem tree, while `\"bare_git_tools\"` allows executing read-only Git query commands.\n- **credential:** Optional secret identifier (`\"workspace-pat\"` or `\"ssh-key\"`) used to authenticate against private upstream repositories without exposing cleartext credentials to agents.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Attaching Workflow Transition Hooks with Dry-Run\n\n1. **Navigate to Workflow Studio:** Open `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows`.\n2. **Select Transition Rule:** Locate the transition rule card for your target state movement (e.g. `In Progress -> Done`).\n3. **Click Attach Hook:** Click the **+ Attach Hook** button to open the Attach Workflow Hook Dialog.\n4. **Define Hook Parameters:** Specify a unique Hook Name (e.g. `reality-checker-done-gate`), select the Hook Kind (`sync_validator`, `sync_mutator`, or `agent_interceptor`), and choose the Failure Policy (`fail_closed` to block unverified tickets, or `fail_open_warn` to log warnings while allowing progress).\n5. **Select Engine or Agent:** For Wasm, choose an approved module (e.g. `check-blockers-resolved`). For Agent Interceptors, select the target Agent Definition from the dropdown; the system automatically populates the agent's real timeout (between 1 and 300,000 ms, e.g. `60000ms`). For mutators, select permitted mutable fields (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, or `custom_fields.<slug>`).\n6. **Save Hook:** Click **Attach Hook** to commit the hook to the project's transition pipeline.\n7. **Simulate in Dry-Run Drawer:** Click the **Dry-Run** button on the rule card, choose a sample ticket fixture or enter a live issue key, click **Run Dry-Run** to execute the pipeline in a simulated, non-mutating sandbox, and inspect the execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms`, `3.4ms`), returned logs, and allowed/vetoed verdicts before deploying to production.\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Binding Git Repositories\n\n1. **Access Repository Bindings:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows` and scroll down to the Git Repository Bindings section.\n2. **Click Bind Repository:** Click **+ Bind Git Repository** to open the repository configuration drawer.\n3. **Configure Repository Details:** Enter the Repository Slug (e.g. `kanban-core`), remote repository URL (`remote_url`), default tracking branch or ref (`default_ref`, e.g. `main`), access mode (`ro_vfs_mount` or `bare_git_tools`), and optional credential binding (`workspace-pat` or `ssh-key`).\n4. **Enforce Read-Only Mirror Invariant:** The system creates a local bare Git mirror with strict read-only permissions (`:ro`). Autonomous agents can query commit trees and run `git diff` during gate reviews, but write access is impossible.\n5. **Trigger Immediate Mirror Refresh:** Click **Refresh Mirror** to perform an initial fetch and verify that commit SHAs and branch references synchronize successfully.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) manages dynamic attributes, safe mathematical formulas, WebAssembly modules, and dual-control tenant scope escalations under AAL2 cryptographic verification.\n\n![Custom Fields Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-desktop-1440x900-dark.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-landscape-1024x768.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-tablet-portrait-768x1024.png)\n![Custom Fields Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice5-custom-fields-studio-mobile-portrait-390x844.png)\n![Custom Fields Editor Tier A](images/slice5-custom-fields-editor-tier-a-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Structured Format](images/slice5-custom-fields-structured-format-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Custom Fields Dual Control Escalation](images/slice5-custom-fields-escalation-dual-control-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable custom field title | String (1 to 80 characters) | Required | Displayed on ticket rails, table columns, and board badges |\n| `slug` | Unique identifier used in formulas and API queries | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and underlying calculation engine | `\"scalar\"`, `\"formula\"`, `\"wasm\"`, `\"nsjail\"` | `\"scalar\"` | Determines execution latency and Standalone mode compatibility |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `\"scalar\"`, `\"wasm\"` (Tier A), `\"nsjail\"` (Tier B) | `\"wasm\"` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Identity token provided during script execution | `\"INVOKING_USER\"`, `\"PROJECT_ADMIN_SP\"`, `\"SERVER_ADMIN_SP\"` | `\"INVOKING_USER\"` | Elevated service principal modes require dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `\"PROJECT\"`, `\"WORKSPACE\"`, `\"SERVER\"` | `\"PROJECT\"` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `\"text\"`, `\"number\"`, `\"badge\"`, `\"severity_pill\"`, `\"url\"`, `\"markdown_snippet\"`, `\"json_table\"` | `\"text\"` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen format | JSON object matching format schema | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Line-delimited (`\\n`) domain list | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n| `approval_status` | Current dual-control escalation state | `\"PENDING\"`, `\"PENDING_SECOND_APPROVER\"`, `\"APPROVED\"`, `\"REVOKED\"` | `\"APPROVED\"` (Project) | Gated at `PENDING` when requesting Workspace or Server scope |\n| `aal2_freshness` | Maximum age of AAL2 verification for approval | Integer (Max 900 seconds / 15 minutes) | 900s | Enforces fresh multi-factor authentication on administrative step-up |\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs (Custom Fields Studio & Dual-Control Escalation)\n\n- **name:** The human-facing label displayed on ticket property rails, table view columns, and board card badges (1 to 80 characters). Rendered alongside a subtle test tube icon (`\ud83e\uddea`) for computed fields.\n- **slug:** The immutable ASCII identifier representing the custom field in JSON payloads, query filters, and Safe Formula references. Derived automatically from the field name upon creation.\n- **flavor:** The computation tier. UI defaults to `\"scalar\"` (`CustomFieldDefinitionEditor.tsx` line 245; stores static values); `\"formula\"` executes deterministic Python AST math in-process (<5ms); `\"wasm\"` runs compiled WebAssembly modules via `wasmtime` (<25ms; API tier default); `\"nsjail\"` executes Linux container scripts (<250ms).\n- **sandbox_tier:** Selects between in-process WebAssembly (`wasm` Tier A) and containerized namespace isolation (`nsjail` Tier B). In Standalone mode, `nsjail` is fail-closed disabled with `SANDBOX_UNAVAILABLE_IN_STANDALONE`.\n- **credential_mode:** Determines which identity credentials are provided to the script during execution. `\"INVOKING_USER\"` executes strictly within the permissions of the calling user. `\"PROJECT_ADMIN_SP\"` executes with Project Admin service principal authority. `\"SERVER_ADMIN_SP\"` executes with Server Admin service principal authority and mandates dual-control approval.\n- **target_scope:** Controls the data visibility boundary of the field. `\"PROJECT\"` scope can only read issues within the active project. Escalating to `\"WORKSPACE\"` (reading across all workspace projects) or `\"SERVER\"` (global) requires dual-control cryptographic sign-off.\n- **output_format:** The strict Pydantic schema used to validate and render the field's computed output. Supports 7 canonical formats: `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, and `json_table`.\n- **format_metadata:** A JSON configuration dictionary customizing the chosen output format (such as specifying `unit_suffix: \"%\"`, `maximum_fraction_digits: 1`, or table column definitions).\n- **script_source:** The executable source code (Python formula expression, Wasm bytecode, or shell script). Hashed with SHA-256 upon save; if the hash in PostgreSQL fails verification at runtime, execution is halted immediately.\n- **egress_allowlist:** A line-delimited (`\\n`) list of permitted network hosts accessible to `nsjail` scripts. By default, network egress is completely disabled via kernel `CLONE_NEWNET`.\n- **approval_status:** Tracks the dual-control lifecycle (`PENDING`, `PENDING_SECOND_APPROVER`, `APPROVED`, `REVOKED`). Fields requesting `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER` scope enter `PENDING` status and cannot execute until approved by an administrator holding target tier authority.\n- **aal2_freshness:** The security policy requiring that the approving administrator must have performed multi-factor authentication (biometric Passkey, hardware key, or TOTP) within the preceding 900 seconds (15 minutes).\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Authoring and Requesting Dual-Control Custom Scripted Fields\n\n1. **Open Custom Fields Studio:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/custom-fields` and click **+ New Custom Field**.\n2. **Define Field Metadata:** Enter a Name (e.g. `Cross-Project Security Compliance Score`), confirm the automatically generated Slug (`cross-proj-sec-score`), select the Flavor (`formula` or `wasm`), and choose the Output Format (`severity_pill` or `number`).\n3. **Request Elevated Scope:** Set Target Scope to `WORKSPACE` or `SERVER`. Optionally select Credential Mode as `PROJECT_ADMIN_SP` or `SERVER_ADMIN_SP` if elevated service authority is required. The UI immediately surfaces an amber Dual-Control Escalation Banner warning of the expanded data access boundary.\n4. **Draft and Test Execute:** Author your formula expression or script logic, then click **Test Execute** to simulate evaluation in memory against sample payloads.\n5. **Submit for Approval:** Click **Save & Request Escalation**. The field enters `pending` approval status.\n6. **Independent AAL2 Review & Approval:** A distinct Workspace or Server Administrator opens the Custom Fields Studio, inspects the script source, requested scope, and blast radius, clicks **Approve Escalation**, and provides fresh Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) credentials (Passkey, security key, or TOTP). Once verified, the field is activated and logged in the immutable audit ledger.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) and Granular RBAC Policy Builder configure enterprise identity providers, Active Directory / LDAPS trees, OpenID Connect SSO, WebAuthn Passkeys, TOTP multi-factor authentication, break-glass lockout prevention, and 30-permission custom roles.\n\n![Admin Authentication Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice1-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_kind` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake, secret custody, and token exchange |\n| `slug` | URL-safe card identifier used in auth routes | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` (3 to 48 chars) | Required | Formats callback and login endpoints (e.g. `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`) |\n| `display_name` | User-facing card title rendered on `/login` screen | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed directly on login buttons and cards |\n| `button_subtext` | Optional explanatory subtext beneath button | UTF-8 String (up to 200 characters) or null | None | Guides users on credential format or organizational tenant |\n| `icon_preset` | Visual icon rendered inside login card button | `key`, `shield`, `google`, `github`, `microsoft`, `ldap`, `terminal` | `key` | Provides immediate visual recognition on `/login` container |\n| `is_enabled` | Master active state toggle for the card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Disabled cards reject login attempts immediately with 403 FORBIDDEN |\n| `sort_rank` | Fractional rank ordering on `/login` screen | Fractional-index midpoint string (e.g. `\"V00V\"`, `\"kV0FV\"`) computed via `midpoint_rank()` | Derived | Lower ranks appear first; allows prioritizing corporate SSO over fallback admin |\n| `auto_provision_users` | Automatic local user creation on first SSO login | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When true, new users are provisioned in workspace with default role |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Domain allowlist for user registration and SSO | Array of domain strings (e.g. `[\"corp.example.com\"]`) | Empty list (Allow all) | Rejects unmatched email claims during OIDC and LDAP authentication |\n| `public_config` | Non-sensitive provider configuration dictionary | JSON object | `{}` | Exposes discovery URLs and client IDs without leaking secrets |\n| `secret` | Sensitive client secret or bind password | Write-only string | None | Encrypted at rest via AES-256-GCM envelope encryption (`MASTER_KEK`) |\n| `google_oauth_json` | Google Cloud Console client secret JSON | Valid JSON string (up to 32,768 characters) | None | Quick-import payload automatically parsed and verified by backend |\n\n\n### Active Directory and LDAPS Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `url` | Directory connection URL with scheme and host | `^(ldaps\\|ldap)://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]{1,5})?$` | Required | Plaintext unencrypted LDAP rejected; requires `ldaps://` or `ldap://` with StartTLS |\n| `transport_mode` | Cryptographic transport security protocol | `ldaps` (implicit TLS on port 636), `starttls` (explicit StartTLS on port 389) | `ldaps` | Enforces TLS handshake before transmitting bind credentials |\n| `ca_cert_pem` | Custom enterprise Certificate Authority bundle | PEM-formatted X.509 certificate string (max 64 KiB) | None | Pins enterprise root CA; rejects untrusted self-signed certificates |\n| `spki_sha256_pins` | Subject Public Key Info SHA-256 pin hashes | Array of 64-hex SHA-256 strings (up to 4 pins) | `[]` | Cryptographically pins public key identity; prevents rogue CA issuance |\n| `service_bind_dn` | Service account Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (up to 512 chars) or null | None | Read-only service principal used for user lookup and group searches |\n| `base_dn` | Directory search subtree root Distinguished Name | LDAP DN string (1 to 512 chars) | Required | Scopes user search to authorized organizational container (e.g. `OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=com`) |\n| `user_search_filter` | LDAP query filter for user account resolution | LDAP filter expression substituting `{username}` | `(&(objectClass=person)(uid={username}))` | Resolves login username to directory user; AD preset uses `sAMAccountName` |\n| `subject_attribute` | Attribute providing immutable user identity | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `entryUUID`, `objectGUID`) | `entryUUID` | Binds external user identity immutably across username renames |\n| `email_attribute` | Attribute containing user email address | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `mail`) | `mail` | Source of verified user email address for workspace mapping |\n| `display_name_attribute` | Attribute containing user full name | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `displayName`, `cn`) | `cn` | Displayed on user profile and activity ledger |\n| `member_of_attribute` | Attribute containing direct group memberships | ASCII attribute name (e.g. `memberOf`) | `memberOf` | Evaluated during group membership extraction and role mapping |\n| `group_dn_map` | Dictionary mapping directory group DNs to roles | JSON dictionary of `{dn: role_slug}` (up to 200 mappings) | `{}` | Automatically provisions mapped workspace and project roles upon login |\n| `access_mode` | Access restriction policy | `any_user` (all valid users), `group_restricted` (member of allowed group) | `any_user` | When restricted, users not in `user_groups` or `admin_groups` are denied entry |\n| `admin_groups` | Directory group names granting admin authority | Array of group names (up to 200 chars each) | `[]` | Grants `SERVER_ADMIN` or `WORKSPACE_ADMIN` to authenticated group members |\n| `user_groups` | Directory group names granting member authority | Array of group names (up to 200 chars each) | `[]` | Grants baseline member access when `access_mode` is `group_restricted` |\n\n\n### OpenID Connect (OIDC) and Google OAuth Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint base URL | Valid HTTPS URL | Required | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic JWKS keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth application client identifier | ASCII string | Required | Identifies application to identity provider authorization server |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth application client secret | Write-only secret string | Required | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never returned in REST responses |\n| `scopes` | OIDC OAuth scope parameters | Array of scope strings in API; comma-separated in drawer | `[\"openid\", \"email\", \"profile\"]` | Must include `openid` and `email` for verified claim extraction |\n| `redirect_uri` | Callback endpoint registered with IdP | Fixed format `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback` | Derived | Enforces strict origin matching against current deployment URL |\n| `require_email_verified` | Rejects unverified IdP email addresses | Boolean (`true`) | `true` | If `email_verified: false`, rejects authentication with 403 EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED |\n\n\n### Personal Security and Multi-Factor Authentication Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | Canonical string `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or relay attacks |\n| `totp_secret` | High-entropy base32 secret for TOTP | Base32 string (RFC 6238) | Generated on enroll | Validates 6-digit one-time codes with 30s step interval and +/- 1 drift |\n| `recovery_codes` | Backup emergency recovery codes | Array of 10 single-use high-entropy recovery codes | 10 codes | Single-use emergency codes stored as salted hashes for break-glass recovery |\n| `websudo_duration_seconds` | Duration of elevated AAL2 administrator session | Integer (seconds) | `900` (15 minutes) | Sliding window for sensitive mutations; explicit drop via `/aal2/drop` |\n| `lockout_prevention_layer` | Anti-lockout verification enforcement | `db_trigger`, `handshake_probe_15m` | Both active | Blocks disabling LOCAL_ADMIN without verified interactive admin login within 15m |\n\n\n### Granular RBAC Policy Builder Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `rbac_scope_tier` | Administrative scope tier of the role | `system admin`, `workspace admin`, `project admin` | `workspace admin` | Governs role hierarchy and locks lower-tier admins from editing parent roles |\n| `role_slug` | Unique lowercase identifier for custom role | Lowercase ASCII `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` | Required | Identifies role in database bindings and audit ledger records |\n| `role_name` | Human-readable role title | UTF-8 String (1 to 100 characters) | Required | Displayed on user management surfaces, member cards, and badges |\n| `color_hex` | UI badge accent color | 7-character hex code (e.g. `#4f46e5`) | `#6b7280` | Distinguishes role visually across member tables and peek drawers |\n| `rbac_permissions` | List of granted granular permissions | Array of permission strings from 30 canonical keys | `[]` | Evaluated with explicit DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`) |\n\n![Custom RBAC Policy Builder - Desktop](images/slice1-custom-rbac-policy-builder.png)\n\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **provider_kind:** Specifies the authentication protocol implemented by the card. Permitted values are `LOCAL_ADMIN` (argon2id hashed platform administrative credentials), `OIDC` (OpenID Connect discovery and authorization code flow), `OAUTH2` (generic OAuth 2.0 provider with userinfo endpoint), and `LDAP` (Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration). The provider kind determines schema validation rules, secret custody requirements, and token minting behavior.\n- **slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the authentication card. Must conform to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` between 3 and 48 characters. The slug forms the path parameter in public login and callback routes, including `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`, `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback`, and `/api/v1/auth/ldap/{slug}/login`. Immutable after creation to maintain bookmark and redirect stability.\n- **display_name:** The user-facing label rendered on the login card button on `/login` (1 to 100 characters). For example, `\"Corporate Active Directory\"` or `\"Google Workspace SSO\"`. Provides clear identification of the identity source for end users.\n- **button_subtext:** Optional secondary guidance rendered in smaller typography directly below the card title (up to 200 characters). Commonly used to specify login formats, such as `\"Use your firstname.lastname@example.com account\"` or `\"Hardware security key required\"`.\n- **icon_preset:** Visual icon token rendered on the authentication card button. Supported values include `key` (generic credential), `shield` (security key / passkey), `google` (Google colored G), `github` (GitHub octocat), `microsoft` (Windows logo), `ldap` (directory tree), and `terminal` (administrative root). Enhances visual scannability on multi-card login surfaces.\n- **is_enabled:** Master toggle controlling whether the authentication card is active on the `/login` screen. When set to `false`, the card is hidden from the login container, and any authentication attempts targeting the card's routes are immediately rejected with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN`. Mutations toggling `is_enabled` enforce anti-lockout validation.\n- **sort_rank:** Fractional-index midpoint string computed via `midpoint_rank()` (e.g. `\"V00V\"`, `\"kV0FV\"`) governing the visual sequence of cards on the `/login` container. Cards with lower lexicographical ranks appear first. Administrators use this setting to position primary corporate SSO providers at the top while keeping break-glass local admin cards accessible at the bottom without needing to renumber surrounding cards.\n- **auto_provision_users:** When enabled (`true`), an authenticated user whose external identity claim is verified but does not yet have an account in \ud83d\udc4dkanban is automatically provisioned with a new local user record and assigned the workspace default role. When disabled (`false`), external users must be explicitly invited before logging in.\n- **allowed_email_domains:** A security restriction array of permitted corporate email domains (e.g., `[\"corp.example.com\", \"subsidiary.com\"]`). During OIDC token exchange or LDAP authentication, if the user's verified email address domain does not match any entry in this list, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `DOMAIN_NOT_ALLOWED`.\n- **public_config:** A JSON dictionary containing non-sensitive provider configuration attributes. Stored in PostgreSQL `jsonb` columns and safe to expose to administrative API callers. Includes discovery URLs, client IDs, LDAP hosts, user search filters, and group mapping definitions.\n- **secret:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private key strings. Inbound payloads accept this write-only parameter, which is immediately encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM envelope encryption with the platform `MASTER_KEK`. The secret is never returned in any REST API response or serialization model.\n- **google_oauth_json:** A convenience payload accepting the raw client secret JSON file downloaded directly from the Google Cloud Console (up to 32,768 characters). The backend parses the JSON, validates client ID, client secret, redirect URI alignment, and populates `public_config` without manual copy-pasting.\n- **url:** The directory connection URL with protocol scheme and hostname matching `^(ldaps|ldap)://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+(:[0-9]{1,5})?$`. Unencrypted plaintext LDAP is strictly rejected; connections must negotiate TLS via `ldaps://` or `ldap://` with StartTLS.\n- **transport_mode:** Transport encryption protocol for directory traffic. Permitted values are `ldaps` (direct TLS handshake upon TCP socket connection on port 636) and `starttls` (explicit StartTLS extended operation negotiation on port 389 prior to issuing bind requests).\n- **ca_cert_pem:** Custom PEM-formatted X.509 certificate authority bundle (up to 64 KiB). When connecting to enterprise directory controllers utilizing private internal PKI, administrators paste the issuing root and intermediate CA certificates here. The LDAP client validates the server certificate strictly against this bundle, rejecting untrusted certificates.\n- **spki_sha256_pins:** Array of Subject Public Key Info (SPKI) SHA-256 hashes (each exactly 64 hexadecimal characters, up to 4 pins). Configured via backend API to cryptographically pin the server certificate public key and block rogue CA or proxy interception.\n- **service_bind_dn:** The Distinguished Name of the service account used by \ud83d\udc4dkanban to execute user lookup and group resolution queries (e.g., `CN=KanbanService,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=com`, up to 512 characters). Must possess read-only search permissions over the target user and group subtrees.\n- **base_dn:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree searched for user accounts (e.g., `OU=Employees,DC=corp,DC=com`, up to 512 characters). Scopes directory queries to prevent scanning unrelated machine or organizational containers.\n- **user_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to resolve the input login username to a specific Active Directory user object. The token `{username}` is safely escaped and substituted into the filter (default `(&(objectClass=person)(uid={username}))`, Active Directory preset `(&(objectCategory=person)(objectClass=user)(sAMAccountName={username}))`).\n- **subject_attribute:** The directory attribute providing an immutable, permanent identifier for the authenticated user (default `entryUUID`, Active Directory uses `objectGUID`). Ensures account continuity even if usernames or email addresses change.\n- **email_attribute:** The directory attribute containing the user's primary email address (default `mail`). Populates the verified user email address upon authentication.\n- **display_name_attribute:** The directory attribute containing the user's human-readable name (default `cn`, Active Directory uses `displayName`). Populates the user's profile display name.\n- **member_of_attribute:** The directory attribute containing direct group memberships on user objects (default `memberOf`). Used during group synchronization to locate groups mapped to \ud83d\udc4dkanban roles.\n- **group_dn_map:** JSON dictionary mapping directory group Distinguished Names to \ud83d\udc4dkanban role slugs (up to 200 mappings). During login, the backend evaluates user group memberships and assigns mapped roles automatically.\n- **access_mode:** Access restriction policy governing directory logins. Permitted values are `any_user` (permits all successfully bound directory users) and `group_restricted` (restricts login to users belonging to specified `user_groups` or `admin_groups`).\n- **admin_groups:** Array of directory group names granting administrative privileges (`SERVER_ADMIN` or `WORKSPACE_ADMIN`). Users belonging to these groups receive elevated administrative access upon login.\n- **user_groups:** Array of directory group names granting member privileges. When `access_mode` is set to `group_restricted`, users must belong to at least one group listed in `user_groups` or `admin_groups` to log in.\n- **issuer_url:** The standard OpenID Connect discovery base URL (e.g., `https://accounts.google.com` or `https://keycloak.corp.example.com/realms/enterprise`). The backend appends `/.well-known/openid-configuration` to retrieve endpoint URLs, signing algorithms, and JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS).\n- **client_id:** The public OAuth client identifier issued by the identity provider during application registration.\n- **client_secret:** The confidential OAuth client secret credential issued by the identity provider. Held strictly in write-only custody and encrypted via AES-256-GCM.\n- **scopes:** Array of requested OAuth scopes in API payloads (`[\"openid\", \"email\", \"profile\"]`), entered as a comma-separated string in the editor drawer. Scopes must guarantee delivery of the `sub` and `email` claims.\n- **redirect_uri:** The immutable callback URI derived from the deployment hostname and card slug: `https://<domain>/api/v1/auth/oidc/<slug>/callback`. Must be registered verbatim in the identity provider console.\n- **require_email_verified:** Mandatory boolean safety check. When true, the ID token claim `email_verified` must be strictly `true`. If the claim is `false` or missing, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED` to prevent email spoofing attacks.\n- **rp_name:** WebAuthn Relying Party display name. Hardcoded to the canonical platform brand `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` across all WebAuthn registration and authentication credential requests. Displayed by operating system biometric dialogs and hardware tokens.\n- **rp_id:** WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier. Formatted as a strict ASCII Fully Qualified Domain Name matching the browser window origin without protocol, port, or trailing slashes (e.g., `kanban.example.com`). Prevents cross-domain credential phishing.\n- **totp_secret:** High-entropy base32 encoded secret key generated during TOTP authenticator enrollment (`POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/enroll`). Encrypted at rest and used to validate 6-digit RFC 6238 time-step codes with a 30-second interval and +/- 1 step drift tolerance.\n- **recovery_codes:** Array of 10 single-use backup recovery codes minted during MFA enrollment (`RECOVERY_CODE_COUNT = 10`). Stored in PostgreSQL exclusively as cryptographic salted hashes. Used for break-glass account recovery when hardware tokens or authenticator apps are unavailable.\n- **websudo_duration_seconds:** The lifetime of an elevated Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) session window (default 900 seconds / 15 minutes). All administrative endpoints (`/api/v1/admin/*`) require active AAL2 verification. When expired, the frontend prompts for step-up elevation via WebSudo without discarding dirty form state.\n- **lockout_prevention_layer:** Multi-layered defense preventing administrative lockouts. Evaluated at both the PostgreSQL trigger layer (`db_trigger`) and application service layer (`handshake_probe_15m`). While test handshakes (`POST /api/v1/admin/auth-cards/test-handshake`) verify endpoint health status, disabling or deleting `LOCAL_ADMIN` strictly requires that an external provider card has an active administrator account mapping AND has recorded a verified interactive `SERVER_ADMIN` login within the last 15 minutes (`last_admin_handshake_at >= clock_timestamp() - interval '15 minutes'`).\n- **rbac_scope_tier:** The administrative boundary defining where a custom RBAC role applies. Allowed values are `system admin` (global platform governance), `workspace admin` (workspace-wide settings, members, and custom fields), and `project admin` (project-specific workflows, WIP limits, and issue operations). Roles in higher tiers cannot be edited by administrators in lower tiers.\n- **role_slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the custom role, conforming to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$`. Identifies the role across role bindings and audit records.\n- **role_name:** The human-readable name of the custom role (1 to 100 characters), rendered in member lists, access matrices, and role assignment pills.\n- **color_hex:** Hexadecimal color string (e.g., `#4f46e5`) utilized for UI badges and role chips across user management and permission tables.\n- **rbac_permissions:** The set of granular permissions assigned to a custom RBAC role, selected from the 30 canonical keys across Workspace (8), Issue (10), and Field (12) tiers. Evaluated alongside the 8-bit capability bitmask with absolute DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`).\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Minting Modal](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `label` | Human-readable label identifying the key or client tool | String (1 to 120 characters) | None (Optional) | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `owner_kind` | Identity persona bound to minted key | `personal_user`, `service_bot` | `personal_user` | Service bot keys enforce strict service account RLS boundaries |\n| `scope_tier` | Tenancy authorization scope boundary | `personal`, `project`, `workspace`, `global` | `workspace` | Restricts object graph access to specified tenant boundary |\n| `expires_in_days` | Key validity lifespan in days | `30`, `60`, `90`, `365`, `0` (Unlimited) | `60` (Studio mint) / `30` (Personal OAuth) | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED`; Unlimited requires explicit risk confirmation |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (0 to 255) | `31` (UI default) / `7` (API default) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 0 rejects all tools with `INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY` |\n| `allowed_tools` | Explicit whitelist of accessible tools | List of tool name strings | None (All tools in surface) | Restricts tool visibility and invocation to specified set |\n| `disabled_tools` | Explicit blacklist of prohibited tools | List of tool name strings | `[]` (Empty) | Blocks invocation of specified tools even if permitted by surface |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n![Connect OAuth App Modal](images/slice3-connect-oauth-modal.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`surface_type`:** Selects the target streamable MCP endpoint. `/mcp/agent` exposes 10 execution and QA tools with a strict token ceiling of <= 1,850 tokens. `/mcp/docs` exposes 3 knowledge and documentation tools capped at <= 1,350 tokens. `/mcp/admin` exposes 12 governance and lifecycle tools capped at <= 2,800 tokens, requiring AAL2 administrative credentials.\n- **`label`:** Human-readable label assigned to the key during creation (1 to 120 characters). Surfaced in audit log feeds, active session inspectors, and the visual key management table to identify the calling entity or developer machine.\n- **`owner_kind`:** Selects between `personal_user` (executing on behalf of an authenticated human user) and `service_bot` (executing as an autonomous background bot). Service bot keys execute under dedicated service accounts with explicit RLS principal contexts.\n- **`scope_tier`:** Binds the minted token to a specific tenant boundary: `personal` (bound to current user), `project` (scoped to a single project key like `KAN`), `workspace` (scoped to all projects in workspace), or `global` (cross-workspace server scope). Project-scoped keys cannot query or mutate tickets in adjacent projects.\n- **`expires_in_days`:** Key expiration duration in days. The UI offers presets for 30 days, 60 days (default in Studio mint modal), 90 days, 365 days (1 year), and `0` (Unlimited). Selecting `Unlimited` triggers an explicit security risk warning checkbox (`confirm_unlimited`) that must be acknowledged before the key can be minted. Expired keys are rejected with `401 UNAUTHORIZED`.\n- **`capability_mask`:** Monotonic 8-bit integer capability bitmask ceiling (0 to 255). A value of `0` (`NONE`) creates a deny-all key where all tool calls are rejected with `isError: true` (`INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY`). A value of `1` (`VIEW`) restricts operations to read-only, `31` (`VIEW | COMMENT | EDIT | TRANSITION | LINK`) grants standard member permissions (UI default), and `255` (`ALL`) enables complete operational privileges. The backend API default is `7` (`VIEW | COMMENT | EDIT`). Enforced via PostgreSQL `SET LOCAL app.credential_boundary`.\n- **`allowed_tools`:** Optional explicit tool allowlist. When specified, only tools in this list may be listed or invoked by the client. Any invocation of unlisted tools is rejected with `TOOL_NOT_PERMITTED`.\n- **`disabled_tools`:** Explicit tool denylist (default empty). Specific tools can be disabled on a per-key basis (e.g. disabling `transition_ticket` for read/comment bots) even if permitted by surface and capability mask.\n- **`allowed_cidrs`:** Optional network boundary allowlist containing comma- or whitespace-separated IPv4 or IPv6 subnets (e.g. `127.0.0.1/32`, `192.168.1.0/24`). Requests originating outside allowed CIDR blocks are rejected immediately with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN_CIDR`.\n\n![MCP Config Snippet and Client Onboarding Modal](images/slice3-mcp-config-snippet-modal.png)\n\n### Connect OAuth App and Client Snippets Modals\n\n- **Connect OAuth App Modal:** Provides one-click visual integration guides and connection snippets for external AI tools:\n  - **Gemini App:** Generates direct OAuth Resource URLs for Google Gemini Connected Apps (`gemini.google.com -> Settings -> Connected Apps -> Add Custom MCP Server`).\n  - **Claude Code & Desktop:** Emits standard `claude_desktop_config.json` stanzas and `claude mcp add` CLI commands.\n  - **VS Code & Cursor:** Emits `mcpServers` configuration blocks with automatic SSE endpoint binding.\n  - **Antigravity CLI:** Emits `agy mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n  - **OpenAI Codex:** Emits `codex mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n- **MCP Config Snippet Modal:** Generates copy-paste configuration snippets for newly minted static API keys across Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, and OpenAI Codex. Includes a write-only custody warning reminding operators that secrets will never be displayed again.\n\n![Workload OIDC Federation Studio](images/slice3-workload-oidc-studio.png)\n\n### Workload Identity Federation Studio (`/settings/mcp` and `/admin/mcp`)\n\nThe Workload Identity Federation Studio allows administrators to author and manage RFC 8693 token exchange policies for headless CI/CD runners:\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive policy identifier | String (1 to 120 characters) | Required | Displayed in workload policy table and audit ledger |\n| `description` | Human-readable explanation of policy purpose | String (up to 500 characters) or None | None | Documents workload integration context and team ownership |\n| `issuer` | OpenID Connect token issuer URL | Strict HTTPS URL (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Validates incoming JWT `iss` claim; plaintext HTTP rejected |\n| `client_id` | Expected audience claim | String (1 to 255 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `aud` claim |\n| `subject_pattern` | Subject matching pattern | Glob or Regex (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `sub` claim (e.g. `repo:org/repo:*`) |\n| `required_claims_json` | Exact claim match constraints | JSON Object | `{}` | Enforces additional claims (e.g. `{\"environment\": \"production\"}`) |\n| `surface` | Target MCP surface | `agent` or `docs` | `agent` | Determines which MCP endpoint the federated token can invoke |\n| `scopes` | Allowed OAuth privilege scopes | List of `SURFACE_SCOPES` strings | `[]` (Empty) | Bounds the granted scopes (e.g. `[\"agent:read\", \"agent:write\"]`) |\n| `allowed_project_ids` | Permitted project UUID boundaries | List of UUIDs or None | None (All accessible) | Limits workload token authority strictly to designated project IDs |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for token exchange | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Restricts token exchange to specific runner network IP blocks |\n| `capability_mask` | Capability bitmask ceiling | Integer between 0 and 255 | 255 | Monotonic capability ceiling enforced by RLS |\n| `max_ttl_seconds` | Lifespan of issued ephemeral token | Integer between 60 and 86400 seconds | 3600 (1 hour) | Limits exposure window of federated workload credentials |\n| `is_enabled` | Policy active toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled policies immediately reject token exchange |\n\n- **Setting-by-Setting Workload Technical Details:**\n  - **`name`:** Descriptive name assigned to the workload identity policy (1 to 120 characters). Displayed in governance tables and audit records.\n  - **`description`:** Optional documentation field (up to 500 characters) explaining the pipeline context, team owner, or workflow triggering the token exchange.\n  - **`issuer`:** The trusted OpenID Connect issuer URL. Must use a strict HTTPS scheme (e.g. `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`). Plaintext HTTP URLs are rejected.\n  - **`client_id`:** Expected audience identifier (`aud`) matching the workload runner configuration (1 to 255 characters).\n  - **`subject_pattern`:** Glob pattern or regex matched against the incoming OIDC JWT `sub` claim (1 to 500 characters). Evaluated using POSIX pattern matching.\n  - **`required_claims_json`:** Arbitrary JSON dictionary of required key-value claims that must match exactly in the incoming token (e.g. branch, environment, or repository slug).\n  - **`surface`:** The target MCP surface granted to the federated token (`agent` or `docs`).\n  - **`scopes`:** List of allowed scopes granted upon successful exchange, restricted strictly to valid surface scopes (such as `agent:read`, `agent:write`, or `docs:read`).\n  - **`allowed_project_ids`:** Optional list of project UUIDs restricting where the exchanged token can query or mutate work items.\n  - **`allowed_cidrs`:** Optional network boundary allowlist restricting token exchange requests to authorized runner subnet IP addresses.\n  - **`capability_mask`:** Monotonic capability bitmask ceiling (0 to 255, default 255). Further constrains the operations permitted by the resulting token.\n  - **`max_ttl_seconds`:** Maximum validity period for issued short-lived access tokens (60 to 86,400 seconds, default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour).\n  - **`is_enabled`:** Administrative master switch for the policy. When set to `false`, token exchange requests matching this policy are rejected immediately.\n- **Built-in Presets:** The Studio provides one-click presets for common automated environments:\n  - **GitHub Actions:** Pre-fills issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com` and pattern `repo:org/repo:ref:refs/heads/*`.\n  - **GitLab CI:** Pre-fills issuer `https://gitlab.com` and pattern `project_path:group/project:ref:*:ref_type:branch`.\n  - **Kubernetes:** Pre-fills in-cluster issuer `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local` and pattern `system:serviceaccount:default:*`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, configures token lifetimes, and manages client revocation.\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Bulk Purge](images/slice3-oauth-governance-bulk-purge.png)\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `client_type` | Client confidentiality tier | `public`, `confidential` | `public` | Confidential clients require client secret authentication |\n| `auth_method` | Client authentication method | `none`, `client_secret_post`, `client_secret_basic`, `private_key_jwt` | `none` for public | Governs how client verifies identity at token endpoint |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforce` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `true` (`S256`) | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent:read`, `agent:write`, `docs:read`, `docs:write`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, `admin:destructive` | `agent:read` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (60 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour, 900 for JWT) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n| `max_state_len` | Maximum length of OAuth state parameter | 4096 characters | 4096 | Supports large serialized state payloads required by Google Gemini |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for client lifecycle view | `all`, `active`, `suspended`, `revoked` | `all` | Isolates active integrations from suspended or revoked clients |\n| `device_code_charset` | RFC 8628 user code alphabet | Base20 consonants (`BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ`) | `BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ` | Excludes vowels (A, E, I, O, U) and numbers (0-9) to eliminate visual ambiguity and accidental profanities |\n| `device_code_ttl` | RFC 8628 user code and device code expiration window | Integer seconds (60 to 900 seconds) | 900 (15 minutes) | Enforces 15-minute expiration window to prevent brute-force enumeration of pending device codes |\n| `device_rate_limit_lockout` | Rate-limit lockout threshold for invalid verification attempts | 5 invalid attempts per 5-minute window | 5 attempts / 300s | Enforces 5-minute freeze upon 5 failed attempts (RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED HTTP 429) |\n| `device_polling_interval` | RFC 8628 CLI token poll cadence interval | Integer seconds (1 to 60 seconds) | 5 seconds | Governs client polling frequency at /oauth/token (slow_down returned if polled faster) |\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Paginated 500 Clients](images/slice3-oauth-governance-paginated-500.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`client_name`:** Human-readable title of the client application (1 to 128 characters). Displayed prominently to users on authorization consent screens and within the administrative governance directory.\n- **`client_id`:** Unique identifier generated upon client registration, prefixed with `client_` (`client_{secrets.token_urlsafe(16)}`). Serves as the public identifier across OAuth 2.1 authorization and device authorization flows.\n- **`client_type`:** Distinguishes between `public` clients (single-page applications, native desktop tools like Cursor or Claude Desktop, and CLI binaries that cannot safeguard secrets) and `confidential` clients (backend server applications capable of storing secrets securely).\n- **`auth_method`:** Defines the client authentication mechanism used at `/oauth/token`. Public clients use `none` with mandatory PKCE `S256`. Confidential clients use `client_secret_post` (secret in POST body), `client_secret_basic` (HTTP Basic authentication header), or `private_key_jwt` (RFC 7523 asymmetric client assertion).\n- **`redirect_uris`:** Exact list of authorized redirect callback URIs. Every entry must be a valid HTTPS URL, with `http://localhost` and `http://127.0.0.1` permitted exclusively for local development and desktop tools. Wildcards and open redirect patterns are strictly rejected.\n- **`pkce_enforce`:** Enforces Proof Key for Code Exchange (RFC 7636). \ud83d\udc4dkanban requires `code_challenge_method = S256` for all authorization code flows. Plain code challenges (`code_challenge_method = plain`) are rejected with `400 invalid_request`.\n- **`allowed_scopes`:** Space- or comma-delimited list of privilege scopes available to the client, strictly adhering to `SURFACE_SCOPES`: `agent:read`, `agent:write`, `docs:read`, `docs:write`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, and `admin:destructive`.\n- **`access_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of minted access tokens (configurable from 60 to 86,400 seconds; default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour for client credentials, 900 seconds / 15 minutes for JWT assertions). Minimizes the window of exposure if a bearer token is intercepted.\n- **`refresh_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of refresh tokens (86,400 to 2,592,000 seconds, default 30 days). Refresh tokens rotate upon every exchange; if a previously used refresh token is presented, the entire token family is revoked to prevent replay attacks.\n- **`max_state_len`:** Defines the maximum permitted length of the OAuth `state` parameter (4,096 characters). Specifically engineered to accommodate complex serialized routing and nonce payloads generated by Google Gemini and enterprise identity gateways. Requests exceeding 4,096 characters are rejected with HTTP 400.\n- **`status_filter`:** Filters displayed clients in the Governance Studio across four lifecycle states: `all` (complete registry), `active` (operational clients), `suspended` (temporarily blocked from token exchange), and `revoked` (permanently disabled).\n- **`device_code_charset`:** Defines the Base20 consonant character set (`BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ`) used to generate 8-character user codes formatted as `XXXX-XXXX`. Vowels (`A`, `E`, `I`, `O`, `U`) and digits (`0`-`9`) are excluded to avoid offensive words and eliminate visual ambiguity.\n- **`device_code_ttl`:** The validity lifetime of issued user codes and device authorization grants (up to 900 seconds / 15 minutes, default 900s). Pending user codes expire after 15 minutes, returning `EXPIRED_CODE` on `/activate` lookup.\n- **`device_rate_limit_lockout`:** Rate limiting protection against automated brute-force user code enumeration. If 5 failed verification attempts occur within a 5-minute rolling window (300 seconds), subsequent lookups are rejected with `RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` (HTTP 429) and a 5-minute freeze.\n- **`device_polling_interval`:** The minimum interval in seconds that headless CLI clients must wait between consecutive polling requests to `/oauth/token` (default 5 seconds). Polling faster than this interval triggers RFC 8628 `slow_down` responses with an increased 5-second backoff penalty.\n\n![Device Activation Page](images/slice3-device-activate-page.png)\n\n### RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Flow and Bulk Purge\n\n- **RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Authorization Flow:** For terminal agents and CLI tools unable to host local redirect servers, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements RFC 8628 device authorization:\n  - **Initiation:** The CLI calls `POST /oauth/device/authorize` with `client_id` and target `resource` (e.g. `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`).\n  - **User Code Formatting:** The server issues an 8-character `user_code` formatted in hyphenated Base20 (`XXXX-XXXX`) using charset `BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ` (excluding vowels `A,E,I,O,U` to avoid offensive words and numbers `0-9` to eliminate visual ambiguity) with a 15-minute expiration (`device_code_ttl = 900`).\n  - **Activation Interface:** The developer visits `/activate`, enters the code, reviews the requested resource and scopes, selects permitted projects, satisfies AAL2 step-up if administrative scopes are requested, and approves the session. A rate-limit lockout guards against brute force (5 failed verification attempts trigger a 5-minute freeze with `RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`).\n  - **Token Exchange:** The CLI polls `POST /oauth/token` with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code` at the configured RFC 8628 polling interval (default 5s) until approval completes, receiving an ephemeral scoped MCP access token.\n- **Single-Transaction Bulk DCR Client Purge:** High-volume test suites and dynamic client registration can create hundreds of orphaned client rows. The Governance Studio includes a **Purge Stale Clients** action (`POST /api/v1/admin/oauth-clients/bulk-delete`). The backend identifies all public DCR clients with zero active grants and zero refresh tokens, deleting up to thousands of stale records in a single atomic database transaction while preserving active production integrations intact.\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai` or `/workspaces/:ws/settings`) configures multi-provider LLM integrations, write-only envelope encryption for API credentials, failover priority ordering, and monthly token budgets with automated circuit breakers.\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-ai-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![AI Provider Editor Drawer - 0 Password Inputs](images/slice2-ai-provider-drawer-create.png)\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_kind` | Underlying AI runtime architecture | `gemini`, `openai`, `ollama` | Required | Governs protocol serialization, tool calling format, and streaming handlers |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable provider label | UTF-8 String (1 to 80 characters) | Required | Displayed on provider cards, failover stack, and token ledger |\n| `base_url` | Upstream API endpoint address | Valid HTTP or HTTPS URL | Provider default | Local Ollama uses `http://127.0.0.1:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Secret cryptographic provider credential | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; zero password inputs in client DOM |\n| `model_allowlist` | Permitted model identifiers for dispatch | Array or comma-separated list of strings | Probed list | Only allowlisted models are accessible for completion and agent execution |\n| `timeout_ms` | Hard ceiling on provider HTTP transactions | Integer between 1000 and 300000 ms | 30000 ms | Upstream requests exceeding timeout fail closed with `GATE_TIMEOUT` (max 300s) |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model sampling | Float between 0.0 and 2.0 | 0.7 | Low values (`0.1` to `0.3`) ensure deterministic verification gate verdicts |\n| `top_p` | Nucleus sampling probability cutoff | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 1.0 | Filters tail tokens; 1.0 evaluates the full cumulative distribution |\n| `max_tokens` | Maximum generation token ceiling | Integer between 1 and 128000 tokens | 4096 | Constrains generation size to avoid runaway completion loops |\n| `mtls_client_cert_pem` | Client certificate for mutual TLS | Valid PEM certificate block or empty | None | Enables certificate-authenticated transport to private gateways |\n| `is_enabled` | Master active toggle for the provider | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled providers are skipped during model probing and runtime dispatch |\n| `period_kind` | Accounting duration for consumption caps | `daily` or `monthly` | `monthly` | Accounting period tier: daily or monthly (resets token consumption counter) |\n| `token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget limit | Non-negative integer (0 to 10000000000) | 10000000000 | `0` enforces Hard AI Freeze; `10000000000` enables Unlimited Quota |\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Unlimited Quota](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-unlimited.png)\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Hard AI Freeze Warning](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-freeze.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: AI Providers and Token Budgets\n\n- **provider_kind:** Identifies the foundational AI provider protocol engine. Supported choices are `gemini` (Google Gemini 2.5 Flash/Pro with native function calling and multimodal spec support), `openai` (OpenAI GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, and compatible gateways implementing standard JSON schema tool definitions), and `ollama` (local air-gapped runtimes for private edge models like Llama 3 and DeepSeek-R1). Selecting a kind binds the request serializer, streaming parser, and error mapper.\n- **display_name:** The descriptive title assigned to the provider instance (for example, \"Google Gemini Workspace\" or \"Local Ollama Runtime\"). Scoped to the workspace or server tier, this label identifies the provider across cards in `/admin/ai`, the failover priority stack, and the audit ledger.\n- **base_url:** The root network URL for provider API communications. Cloud providers require secure HTTPS endpoints (`https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com` or `https://api.openai.com/v1`), whereas local Ollama deployments typically connect over internal loopback (`http://127.0.0.1:11434`). Outbound calls are subject to network policy and workspace air-gap restrictions.\n- **api_key:** The secret API key used to authenticate upstream cloud requests. Submitted over TLS, stored encrypted at rest via PostgreSQL envelope encryption, and never echoed back to client browsers. In `AiProviderEditorDrawer`, input fields adhere to strict security invariants by using standard masked inputs with 0 `input[type=\"password\"]` elements, preventing browser credential managers from storing or sniffing AI keys.\n- **model_allowlist:** An explicit allowlist of model names permitted for execution under this provider configuration (e.g. `[\"gemini-2.5-flash\", \"gemini-2.5-pro\"]` or `[\"llama3.2:latest\"]`). Administrators can manually specify identifiers or click `Probe Models` to automatically query the upstream provider API and select discovered models via interactive search chips.\n- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock request duration allowed for provider calls, bounded between 1,000 ms and 300,000 ms (default 30,000 ms, with a 300-second reasoning ceiling). When combined with agent execution, the runtime enforces `min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. If the upstream model exceeds this cutoff, the system aborts execution, fails closed, and records `GATE_TIMEOUT` without hanging worker threads.\n- **temperature:** Governs generation randomness on a scale from 0.0 to 2.0 (default 0.7). Lower temperatures (0.1 to 0.3) provide highly deterministic outputs recommended for code review, transition gates, and structured JSON parsing, while moderate values (0.7 to 1.0) support creative sticky synthesis and brainstorming.\n- **top_p:** Controls nucleus sampling by restricting generation to tokens comprising the top P probability mass (0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0). Decreasing this value eliminates low-probability generation branches, enhancing predictability in mission-critical QA workflows.\n- **max_tokens:** The upper bound on token output generated in a single completion or reasoning turn (configurable from 1 to 128,000 tokens, default 4,096). Protects against unbounded model loops and limits context window consumption.\n- **mtls_client_cert_pem:** An optional client certificate in PEM format used when establishing mutual TLS connections to enterprise AI gateways or private proxy infrastructure.\n- **is_enabled:** A boolean toggle that immediately activates or deactivates the provider without deleting its configuration or keys. Inactive providers are bypassed during live completion dispatch and sparkle routing.\n- **priority_and_order:** The failover dispatch sequence determined via `PUT /api/v1/admin/ai/providers/order` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/order`). Providers are evaluated sequentially by priority. If a higher-tier provider encounters a 5xx error or rate limit, the router seamlessly falls back to the next active provider in the stack.\n- **token_cap_and_budgets:** Managed via `PUT /api/v1/admin/ai/budgets` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/budgets`) across daily or monthly periods (`period_kind`). Setting `token_cap = 0` triggers a Hard AI Freeze, immediately rejecting reservations with `BudgetExceeded` and halting Sparkle completions with status `budget`. Selecting Unlimited Quota configures an internal ceiling of 10,000,000,000 tokens, recording ledger telemetry without automated cutoffs. Quick presets provide standard allowances of 1M, 5M, 25M, and 100M tokens.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents` or `/projects/:proj/settings/agents`) configures autonomous agent personas, tool permission boundaries, execution harnesses, and developer onboarding prompts.\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `slug` | Unique URL-safe agent identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (1 to 64 chars) | Required | Used in ticket `@mentions`, API URLs, and activity logs |\n| `display_name` | Human-readable name of the agent | UTF-8 String (1 to 200 characters) | Required | Displayed on ticket assignment cards and inspection drawers |\n| `harness_kind` | Runtime execution environment | `in_app_sdk`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `in_app_sdk` | Governs execution boundary, isolation model, and process privileges |\n| `provider_kind` | AI provider bound to the agent | `gemini`, `openai`, `ollama` | Provider default | Used when `harness_kind = in_app_sdk` to route LLM queries |\n| `model_id` | Model slug for agent reasoning | Valid model identifier string | Provider default | Controls reasoning depth and token processing speed |\n| `cli_binary_allowlist_id`| Pre-approved binary for CLI pipe harness | `agy`, `codex` | `agy` | Zero-shell execution over stdin/stdout inside hardened Linux jail |\n| `cli_args` | Command-line arguments for CLI pipe | Array of string arguments | `[\"-p\"]` | Structured arguments passed directly via execve (never shell) |\n| `remote_runner_pool_slug`| Target pool for remote worker dispatch | Valid pool slug identifier | `default` | Routes heavy build tasks to distributed mTLS worker nodes |\n| `repo_access_mode` | Repository filesystem access permissions | `none`, `ro_vfs_mount`, `bare_git_tools` | `none` | `ro_vfs_mount` mounts Git repo read-only; prevents code tampering |\n| `mcp_allowed_tools` | Permitted Model Context Protocol tools | Array of tool names | Basic read set | Restricts capabilities to least privilege allowlist (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`) |\n| `system_instructions_md`| Persona prompt and operational charter | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown | Required | Directs reasoning behavior and verification requirements |\n| `timeout_ms` | Maximum execution time before cancellation | Integer between 1000 and 300000 ms | 60000 ms | Hard cutoff ceiling; uncompleted tasks fail closed safely (max 300s) |\n| `project_id` | Project scoping and tenancy binding | Valid UUID or null | Null (Workspace) | Enforces PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` tenant isolation |\n| `agent_onboarding_prefs`| User preferences for agent onboarding | JSON object | `{}` | Stores execution mode, model strategy, and QA rigor from `/llms.txt` |\n\n![Agent Validation Modal - 4-Section Trace](images/slice2-agent-validation-modal-trace.png)\n\n![Agent Onboarding Card - Desktop Light](images/slice2-onboarding-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: Agent Definitions and Onboarding\n\n- **slug:** The immutable machine identifier for the agent definition (e.g. `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`). Constrained to lowercase alphanumeric ASCII characters and hyphens. The slug forms the primary address for ticket comments, mention notifications, and REST endpoint routes (`/api/v1/admin/agent-definitions/:slug`).\n- **display_name:** The human-facing label rendered on board cards, ticket assignment selectors, peek drawers, and audit trails. Allows teams to provide intuitive persona titles (e.g. \"Forensic QA Reality Checker\" or \"Architecture Auditor\").\n- **harness_kind:** Specifies the runtime execution architecture. `in_app_sdk` executes in-process using configured cloud or local LLM providers. `cli_pipe` executes external command-line binaries (`agy`, `codex`) inside a zero-shell Linux namespace jail (`nsjail`), communicating strictly over standard input/output with 512 MB memory caps and zero network access. `remote_runner` dispatches tasks to distributed worker daemons connected over mutual TLS.\n- **provider_kind and model_id:** Applicable to `in_app_sdk` harnesses. Specifies the active provider backend (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`) and specific model (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`, `llama3.2:latest`) utilized for the agent persona's completion and tool-calling cycles.\n- **cli_binary_allowlist_id and cli_args:** Governs `cli_pipe` execution. Restricts binary invocations to pre-vetted host binaries (`agy`, `codex`). Arguments are submitted as an array of safe flags (default `[\"-p\"]`) executed directly via `execve`, eliminating shell injection hazards (`/bin/sh` is never spawned).\n- **remote_runner_pool_slug:** Designates the distributed worker pool (such as `default` or `gpu-compute`) responsible for accepting tasks when `harness_kind = remote_runner`.\n- **repo_access_mode:** Enforces Git repository access boundaries during task execution. In `none` mode, the agent operates solely on ticket prose. In `ro_vfs_mount` mode, the target repository is mounted strictly read-only (`CLONE_NEWNS`), allowing static code analysis while preventing unauthorized workspace writes. In `bare_git_tools` mode, read-only bare Git inspection tools are provisioned in an isolated tmpfs directory.\n- **mcp_allowed_tools:** An array of tool names defining the explicit Model Context Protocol allowlist for the agent. Tools outside this list (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`) are hidden from the agent's schema, enforcing strict privilege separation (submitting unlisted tools fails with HTTP 422 `HARNESS_FIELDS_INVALID`).\n- **system_instructions_md:** The core persona prompt formatted in canonical UTF-8 Markdown. Directs agent analysis, defines verification standards, and specifies output formats. Server-side sanitization strips lone surrogates and harmful prompt injection vectors before database storage.\n- **timeout_ms:** Maximum allowable execution time for agent invocations, configurable from 1,000 ms to 300,000 ms (default 60,000 ms, adhering to the 300-second reasoning ceiling from KAN-430). Tasks that exceed this threshold are terminated immediately, preserving thread pools and avoiding stuck workflows.\n- **project_id and rls_isolation:** Binds the agent definition to a specific project UUID or leaves it at workspace scope. PostgreSQL `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` strictly isolates project agents: administrators and members of other projects cannot view, mutate, or validate cross-project agents (returning HTTP 404 Not Found).\n- **agent_validation_modal:** Invoked via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-definitions/:id:validate` or `POST /api/v1/agent-definitions/:id:validate`. Executes a live non-destructive test invocation returning a 4-section forensic verification report: 1. `=== 1. RENDERED CONTEXT ===`, 2. `=== 2. TOOL TRACE ===`, 3. `=== 3. LATENCY & TOKENS ===`, and 4. `=== 4. SCHEMA & POLICY STATUS ===`.\n- **llms_txt_and_onboarding_card:** Machine-readable agent capabilities and skill manifests served at `/llms.txt`. The onboarding card surfaces the canonical onboarding prompt that guides external agents (Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI) through an interactive setup interview, persisting user preferences (`execution_mode`, `model_strategy`, `qa_rigor`, `custom_notes`) to `agent_onboarding_prefs` in `/api/v1/users/me/preferences` strictly within the local project folder without polluting global system paths.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) monitors distributed worker nodes, mints single-use enrollment tokens, and manages hardware-bound mutual TLS (mTLS) identities.\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `hostname` | Unique network hostname of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health cards and active execution telemetry |\n| `pool_slug` | Resource pool grouping for runner fleet | Lowercase ASCII slug | `default` | Allows target assignment of jobs to specialized hardware fleets |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic enrollment token | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires in 60s to 900s (max 15 min); burns upon first enrollment |\n| `client_cert_sha256_hex`| SHA-256 fingerprint of client certificate | 64-char hexadecimal string | Computed at enroll | Binds hardware identity to mTLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `cert_expires_at` | Client certificate expiration timestamp | ISO-8601 Timestamp | 30 days from mint | Enforces periodic hardware credential rotation |\n| `status` | Current operational connectivity state | `online`, `offline`, `revoked` | `offline` | Only `online` runners receive dispatched build and verification jobs |\n| `last_heartbeat_at` | Timestamp of most recent daemon ping | ISO-8601 Timestamp or null | Null | Heartbeat frame received every 15s; missing 45s marks runner offline (`DEAD_EVICTION_S = 45.0`) |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Canonical enrollment CLI invocation | Shell command string | Auto-generated | `echo \"$TOKEN\" | uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `state_dir_permissions` | POSIX permissions on runner daemon host | Octal mode (`0o700`, `0o600`, `0o644`) | Strict defaults | `0o700` state dir, `0o600` `client.key` & `ca.pem`, `0o644` `client.crt` |\n| `mtls_pop_challenge` | Challenge format for Proof-of-Possession | Text challenge string | Auto-generated | Syntax: `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}` |\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal](images/slice2-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: Remote Runners Fleet\n\n- **hostname and pool_slug:** The runner hostname uniquely identifies the physical or virtual execution host (for example, `worker-01.internal.test`). The pool slug groups runners into specialized task fleets (such as `default` or `gpu-compute`), enabling workflows to direct heavy verification suites to properly provisioned worker infrastructure.\n- **enrollment_token:** Ephemeral, single-use authentication token minted via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/enrollment-tokens`. Token lifetime is strictly capped between 60 and 900 seconds (maximum 15 minutes). The token is consumed immediately upon successful certificate signing; replaying an expired or consumed token is rejected with HTTP 401 `ENROLLMENT_TOKEN_INVALID`.\n- **client_cert_sha256_hex:** The cryptographic SHA-256 digest of the X.509 client certificate generated during runner daemon enrollment. The orchestrator registers this fingerprint in the database, verifying that incoming mutual TLS connections match the approved hardware certificate.\n- **cert_expires_at:** The expiration timestamp for the signed client certificate (standard 30-day validity). When a certificate approaches expiration, the runner must be re-enrolled using a freshly minted token.\n- **status and heartbeats:** Reflects runner operational status (`online`, `offline`, or `revoked`). Active daemons send periodic `HeartbeatFrame` packets every 15 seconds over the established mTLS WebSocket channel. If no heartbeat is received within 45 seconds (`DEAD_EVICTION_S = 45.0`), the runner transitions to `offline`, and pending tasks are rescheduled.\n- **unprivileged_daemon_and_posix_permissions:** The runner daemon (`kanban-agent-runner`) executes as an unprivileged, non-root system account. Local cryptographic assets are created with strict POSIX file permissions: state directory `0o700` (`drwx------`), private key `client.key` `0o600` (`-rw-------`), root CA bundle `ca.pem` `0o600` (`-rw-------`), and client certificate `client.crt` `0o644` (`-rw-r--r--`).\n- **mtls_pop_handshake:** Mutual TLS connections connect to dedicated port `:8444` (or WebSocket route `/api/v1/agent-runners/ws`). During handshake, the orchestrator issues a `ChallengeHelloFrame` containing an ephemeral nonce. The runner signs the canonical challenge payload `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}` using its private EC key, proving certificate possession before transitioning to `online`.\n- **runner_revocation:** Administrators can instantly revoke rogue or decommissioned runners via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/:id:revoke`. Revocation immediately terminates active WebSocket connections and flags the certificate fingerprint as revoked. Subsequent connection attempts are rejected with HTTP 403 Forbidden.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed in place. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
   },
   {
     "slug": "system-topology-and-multi-container-architecture",
